(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["3-carthage-cyprian"]=[["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p1","The Councils of Carthage","Although the councils of Carthage which we gather here did not agitate the same question of rebaptism, yet we have judged that all should be published as if into one body, since they were held at nearly the same times, under the same Supreme Pontiff, by the same Cyprian, from almost the same bishops.","Etsi quae eo loci colligimus concilia Carthaginensia non eamdem rebaptismatis quaestionem agitaverint, cuncta tamen quasi in unum corpus edenda esse autumavimus, ut pote iisdem fere temporibus, sub eodem summo Pontifice, ab eodem Cypriano, ex iisdem propemodum episcopis habita."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p2","The Councils of Carthage","CARTHAGINIAN COUNCIL UNDER CYPRIAN THIRD, HELD IN THE YEAR AFTER THE BIRTH OF CHRIST 253. CONCERNING INFANTS TO BE BAPTIZED.","CARTHAGINENSE CONCILIUM SUB CYPRIANO TERTIUM ANNO POST CHRISTUM NATUM CCLIII HABITUM. DE INFANTIBUS BAPTIZANDIS."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p3","Prooemium.","This is the seventh Carthaginian council under Cyprian, as Harduin admonishes, celebrated in the year of Christ 253; but Pagius thinks it fictitious, to the year of Christ 254, n. 5, whom consult., with persecution ceasing, to compose the shaken Church, Cyprian and his fellow bishops of the African church, according to custom, convened; partly to preserve right faith and catholic unity, partly also to restore collapsed ecclesiastical discipline, and to inform the good morals of the faithful. In this synod it was first constituted, that that deacon, whom Rogatianus had denounced before the Fathers of the council concerning his protervity and insults brought against him, should either abstain, or, unless he amend his sin, be altogether deposed. The same he willed to be constituted concerning other protervous and contumelious clerics: because contumacy in subject clerics is perilous, from which they have been accustomed to slip into heresy for the most part. The words of Cyprian in reply to Rogatianus writing concerning this matter are these: \"These are the beginnings of heretics and the origin and attempts of schismatics, of those thinking evil, that they please themselves, that they despise the prelate with proud swelling. Thus the Church is departed from: thus a profane altar is placed outside: thus against the peace of Christ and the ordinance and unity of God it is rebelled. But if he shall have exasperated you further by his contumelies and provoked you, exercise concerning him the power of your honor, that you either depose him or abstain him.\" Secondly, in this same synod, the fathers of the council understanding that a certain Christian, Geminius Victor, departing from this life, had named a certain Fortunatus, a presbyter, as guardian of his sons in his will, and thus, according to the decree of some council long ago celebrated concerning this matter in Africa under predecessors, he had incurred the penalty of excommunication; they announce that the deceased Geminius himself, because he had called away a presbyter from the cult of God to secular things, is excommunicated; therefore they most severely forbid that sacrifice be offered for the deceased, that his name be named at the altar; and they confirm the decree of their predecessors by this synodal constitution. These things are established from Cyprian's Epistle 66, in which he says thus: \"We were gravely moved, I and my colleagues who were present, and our fellow-presbyters who sat with us,\" etc. Baronius, year 257, numbers 13 and 14. In this same council, it seems that it was answered to the doubt of some that the baptism of infants, in case of necessity, ought to be hastened before the eighth day; especially because that council, celebrated in time of a peaceful Church, not under Lucius, as the Venetian edition has, but rather around the beginning of Valerian, may be certainly gathered. What that controversy concerning the baptism of infants was, may be known from Cyprian's Epistle 59 to Fidus the presbyter, here subjoined.","Est hoc concilium Carthaginense VII sub Cypriano ut admonet Harduinus, celebratum anno Christi CCLIII; fictitium autem illud putat Pagius ad ann. Chr. 254, n. 5; quem consule., persecutione cessante, ad quassatam Ecclesiam componendam, Cyprianus ejusque collegae episcopi Africanae ecclesiae, ex more convenerunt; partim ut rectam fidem et catholicam unitatem conservarent, partim etiam ut collapsam ecclesiasticam disciplinam restituerent, ac bonos mores fidelium informarent. In hac synodo constitutum est primo, ut diaconus ille, quem Rogatianus de protervia et conviciis sibi illatis coram Patribus concilii denuntiaverat, aut abstineatur, aut, nisi peccatum emendet, omnino deponatur. Idem de reliquis protervis et contumeliosis clericis constitutum esse volens: quia contumacia in clericis subjectis periculosa sit, ex qua ut plurimum in haeresim prolabi consueverunt. Verba Cypriani responsoria ad Rogatianum hac de re scribentis haec sunt: «Haec sunt initia haereticorum et ortus atque conatus schismaticorum male cogitantium, ut sibi placeant, ut praepositum superbo tumore contemnant. Sic de Ecclesia receditur: sic altare profanum foris collocatur, sic contra pacem Christi et ordinationem atque unitatem Dei rebellatur. Quod si ultra te contumeliis suis exacerbaverit et provocaverit, fungeris circa eum potestate honoris tui, ut eum vel deponas vel abstineas.» Secundo hac eadem synodo patres concilii intelligentes quod Geminius Victor Christianus ex hac vita discedens Fortunatum quemdam presbyterum testamento suorum filiorum tutorem nominasset, atque ita, juxta decretum concilii alicujus, olim hac de re in Africa sub antecessoribus celebrati, poenam excommunicationis incurrisset; ipsum Geminium defunctum, eo quod presbyterum a cultu Dei ad saecularia avocasset, excommunicatum esse denuntiant; ideoque pro defuncto sacrificium offerri, nomen apud altare nominari severe admodum interdicunt; ipsumque antecessorum suorum decretum hac synodali constitutione confirmant. Haec constant ex Cypriani epistola 66, qua sic ait: Graviter commoti sumus, ego et collegae mei, qui presentes aderant, et compresbyteri nostri qui nobis assidebant, etc. Baron. anno 257, numero 13 et 14. Hoc eodem concilio Baptismum infantulorum in casu necessitatis ante octavum diem accelerandum esse, ad quorumdam dubitationem responsum esse videtur: maxime, quia concilium illud tempore Ecclesiae pacato celebratum, non sub Lucio, ut Veneta editio habet, sed potius circa initium Valeriani habitum fuisse, certo colligere liceat. Qualis illa de Baptismo infantulorum controversia fuerit, ex LIX epistola Cypriani ad Fidum presbyterum hic subjecta cognoscere licet."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p4","Prooemium.","This Synodal Epistle. Cyprian and the other colleagues who were present at the council, number LXVI, to Fidus the brother, greeting.","Synodica haec Epistola. Cyprianus et caeteri collegae qui in concilio affuerunt numero LXVI, Fido fratri salutem."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p5","Prooemium.","ARGUMENTUM.— The African Fathers establish that peace rashly granted by Therapius is valid.—The baptism of infants just born is established by the same.—A superstitious custom of not admitting infants to a kiss before the eighth day is noted.—No one is to be hindered from baptism.","ARGUMENTUM.— Patres africani pacem a Therapio temere concessam, ratam esse statuunt.—Infantium modo natorum Baptismus ab iisdem stabilitur.—Superstitiosus mos infantes ad osculum non admittere ante diem octavum notatur.—Nemo a Baptismo impediendus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p6","I.","We have read your letters, most dear brother, by which you signified concerning Victor, a certain former presbyter, that Therapius, our colleague, rashly gave peace to him before he had fully performed penance and had satisfied the Lord God, against whom he had offended, at an unripe time and with premature haste. This matter moved us sufficiently, that there had been a departure from the authority of our decree, that before a legitimate and full time of satisfaction, and without the seeking and conscience of the people, no infirmity pressing nor necessity compelling, peace was granted to him. But after we had long weighed the matter with counsel, we decided that it was sufficient to rebuke our colleague Therapius, because he had done this rashly, and to instruct him that he should not do such a thing in the future. However, peace once given, in whatever way, by a priest of God, we did not think it was to be taken away; and through this we allowed Victor to use the communion granted to him.","Legimus litteras tuas, frater charissime, quibus significasti de Victore, quondam presbytero, quod ei, antequam poenitentiam plenam egisset, et Domino Deo, in quem deliquerat, satisfecisset, temere Therapius, collega noster, immaturo tempore et praepropera festinatione pacem dederit. Quae res nos satis movit, recessum esse a decreti nostri auctoritate, ut ante legitimum et plenum tempus satisfactionis, et sine petitu et conscientia plebis, nulla infirmitate urgente ac necessitate cogente, pax ei concederetur, sed librato apud nos diu consilio, satis fuit objurgare Therapium collegam nostrum quod temere hoc fecerit et instruxisse ne quid tale de caetero faciat. Pacem tamen quomodocumque a sacerdote Dei semel datam non putavimus auferendam, ac per hoc Victori communicationem sibi concessam usurpare permisimus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p7","II.","But as concerns the matter of infants, whom you said ought not to be baptized within the second or third day after they are born, and that the law of ancient circumcision (Gen 17:12) is to be considered, that you thought one who is born should not be baptized and sanctified within the eighth day, far otherwise seemed to all in our council. For in this thing which you thought should be done, no one consented, but we all rather judged that the mercy and grace of God is to be denied to no man born. For since the Lord says in His Gospel: \"The Son of man came not to destroy the souls of men, but to save\" (Lk 9:56), as far as in us is, if it can be done, no soul is to be lost. For what is lacking to him who has once been formed in the womb by the hands of God? For in our eyes and according to the course of worldly days, those who are born seem to receive increase. But whatever is made by God is perfected by the majesty and work of God the maker.","Quantum vero ad causam infantium pertinet, quos dixisti intra secundum vel tertium diem quo nati sint constitutos baptizari non oportere, et considerandam esse legem circumcisionis antiquae, ut intra octavum diem eum qui natus est baptizandum et sanctificandum non putares, longe aliud in concilio nostro omnibus visum est. In hoc enim quod tu putabas esse faciendum nemo consensit, sed universi potius judicavimus nulli homini nato misericordiam Dei et gratiam denegandam. Nam cum Dominus in Evangelio suo dicat: Filius hominis non venit animas hominum perdere, sed salvare, quantum in nobis est, si fieri potest, nulla anima perdenda est. Quid enim ei deest qui semel in utero Dei manibus formatus est? Nobis enim atque oculis nostris secundum dierum saecularium cursum accipere qui nati sunt incrementum videntur. Caeterum quaecumque a Deo fiunt Dei factoris majestate et opere perfecta sunt."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p8","III.","Finally, the faith of the divine Scriptures declares to us that among all, whether infants or older, there is one equality of the divine gift. Elisha, placing himself upon the infant son of the widow who lay dead, prayed to God and stretched himself out, so that head was applied to head, and face to face, and the limbs of Elisha were applied to each of the infant's limbs, and feet were joined to feet (2 Kings 4:34). Which thing, if it be thought according to the inequality of our birth and body, an infant could not be equaled to an adult and grown man, nor could small limbs cohere and suffice for larger ones. But there the divine and spiritual equality is expressed, that all men are equal and alike when they have been once made by God, and our age can have a difference in the increments of the body according to the world, not according to God; unless perhaps even the grace itself which is given to the baptized is given less or greater according to the age of the recipients, whereas the Holy Spirit is given not in measure, but from paternal piety and indulgence is equally provided to all. For God, as He does not accept person, so He does not accept age, when He shows Himself a father to all, with equal balance, for the attainment of heavenly grace.","Esse denique apud omnes, sive infantes, sive majores natu, unam divini muneris aequalitatem declarat nobis divinae Scripturae fides. Helisaeus super infantem viduae filium qui mortuus jacebat ita se Deum deprecans superstravit, ut capiti caput et faciei facies applicaretur, et superfusi Helisaei membra singulis parvuli membris et pedes pedibus jungerentur. Quae res si secundum nativitatis nostrae et corporis inaequalitatem cogitetur, adulto et provecto infans non posset aequari, nec cohaerere et sufficere possent parva membra majoribus. Sed illic aequalitas divina et spiritalis exprimitur, quod pares atque aequales sint omnes homines, quando a Deo semel facti sunt, et possit aetas nostra in incrementis corporis secundum saeculum, non secundum Deum habere discrimen; nisi si et gratia ipsa, quae baptizatis datur, pro aetate accipientium vel minor vel major tribuitur, cum Spiritus sanctus non de mensura, sed de pietate atque indulgentia paterna aequalis omnibus praebeatur. Nam Deus ut personam non accipit, sic nec aetatem, cum se omnibus ad coelestis gratiae consecutionem aequalitate librata praebeat patrem."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p9","IV.","For what you said, that an infant in the first days of his birth is not clean, and that each of us would still shudder to kiss him, we think that this ought not to be an impediment to giving heavenly grace. For it is written: \"All things are clean to the clean\" (Tit 1:15). Nor should any of us shudder at what God has deigned to make. For even if an infant is new from birth, yet it is not such that one should shudder to kiss him in giving grace and in making peace, when in kissing the infant each of us ought, according to his religion, to think of the very recent hands of God, which we embrace in the human just now formed, when we embrace what God has made. For as for what in the carnal circumcision the eighth day was observed, it was a sacrament in shadow and figure sent beforehand, but with Christ coming it was fulfilled in truth. For the eighth day, that is, the first day after the sabbath, was about to be the day on which the Lord would rise, and would give life to us and give us spiritual circumcision; this eighth day, that is, after the sabbath the first and the Lord's day, went before in figure. Which figure ceased when afterwards truth came and spiritual circumcision was given to us.","Nam et quod vestigium infantis in primis partus sui diebus constituti mundum non esse dixisti, quod unusquisque nostrum adhuc horreat exosculari, nec hoc putamus ad coelestem gratiam dandam impedimento esse oportere. Scriptum est enim: Omnia munda sunt mundis. Nec aliquis nostrum id debet horrere quod Deus dignatus est facere. Nam etsi adhuc infans a partu novus est, non ita est tamen ut quisquam illum in gratia danda atque in pace facienda horrere debeat osculari, quando in osculo infantis unusquisque nostrum pro sua religione ipsas adhuc recentes Dei manus debeat cogitare, quas in homine modo formato exosculamur quando id quod Deus fecit amplectimur. Nam quod in Judaica circumcisione carnali octavus dies observabatur, sacramentum est in umbra atque in imagine ante praemissum, sed veniente Christo veritate completum. Nam qui octavus dies, id est, post sabbatum primus dies, futurus erat quo Dominus resurgeret, et nos vivificaret et circumcisionem nobis spiritalem daret; hic dies octavus, id est, post sabbatum primus et dominicus, praecessit in imagine. Quae imago cessavit superveniente postmodum veritate et data nobis spiritali circumcisione."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p10","V.","Wherefore we think that no one is to be hindered from obtaining grace by that law which has already been established, nor is spiritual circumcision to be hindered by carnal circumcision, but absolutely every man is to be admitted to the grace of Christ, since Peter also speaks in the Acts of the Apostles and says: \"The Lord told me to call no man common or unclean\" (Acts 10:28). Moreover, if something could impede men from attaining grace, older and grown men and those more advanced in age could be more impeded by graver sins. Moreover, if even to the most grievous offenders and to those who have previously sinned much against God, when they afterward believe, remission of sins is given, and no one is forbidden from baptism and grace, how much more ought an infant not be forbidden, who, newly born, has not sinned, except that, carnally born after Adam, he has contracted the contagion of ancient death by his first birth, and who for the remission of sins comes more easily to this very thing because they are remitted to him not his own but others' sins.","Propter quod neminem putamus a gratia consequenda impediendum esse ea lege quae jam statuta est, nec spiritalem circumcisionem impediri carnali circumcisione debere, sed omnem omnino hominem admittendum esse ad gratiam Christi, quando et Petrus in Actis apostolorum loquatur et dicat: Dominus mihi dixit neminem hominem communem dicendum et immundum. Caeterum si homines impedire aliquid ad consecutionem gratiae posset, magis adultos et provectos et majores natu possent impedire peccata graviora. Porro autem si etiam gravissimis delictoribus, et in Deum multum ante peccantibus, cum postea crediderint, remissa peccatorum datur, et a baptismo atque a gratia nemo prohibetur, quanto magis prohiberi non debet infans, qui recens natus nihil peccavit, nisi quod secundum Adam carnaliter natus contagium mortis antiquae prima nativitate contraxit, qui ad remissam peccatorum hoc ipso facilius accedit quod illi remittuntur, non propria, sed aliena peccata."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p11","VI.","And therefore, most dear brother, this was our sentence in the council, that by us no one is to be forbidden from baptism and from the grace of God, who is merciful and benign and pious to all. Which, since it is to be observed and retained concerning all, we think it is especially to be observed concerning infants themselves and those newly born, who by this very thing more deserve from our help and from divine mercy, that in the first moment of their birth, weeping and wailing, they do nothing else than pray. We wish you, most dear brother, always to be well.","Et idcirco, frater charissime, haec fuit in concilio nostra sententia, a Baptismo atque a gratia Dei, qui omnibus misericors et benignus et pius est, neminem per nos debere prohiberi. Quod cum circa universos observandum sit atque retinendum, tum magis circa infantes ipsos et recens natos observandum putamus, qui hoc ipso de ope nostra ac de divina misericordia plus merentur quod in primo statim nativitatis suae ortu plorantes ac flentes nihil aliud faciunt quam deprecantur. Optamus te, frater charissime, semper bene valere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p12","VI.","CARTHAGINIAN COUNCIL UNDER CYPRIAN FOURTH, HELD IN THE YEAR AFTER THE BIRTH OF CHRIST 254. CONCERNING BASILIDES AND MARTIALIS, BISHOPS OF SPAIN, WHO WERE LIBELLATICI.","CARTHAGINENSE CONCILIUM SUB CYPRIANO QUARTUM. ANNO POST CHRISTUM NATUM CCLIV HABITUM. DE BASILIDE ET MARTIALE HISPANIAE EPISCOPIS LIBELLATICIS."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p13","Prooemium.","This council was held in Africa, in the year of Christ 258, at the instance of the bishops of Spain, to this end, that they might know the judgment of the African church concerning the cause of Basilides and Martialis. The cause was such: Basilides of the church of Legio and Martialis of the church of Asturica, bishops, because in the persecution they had been lapsed, libellatici—namely, who, privately denying the faith, had redeemed public worship of idols with money—were despoiled of episcopal honor upon the denunciation of the faithful of Spain, and Sabinus and Felix were substituted in their place. When the persecution ceased, they, just as before Felicissimus in Africa, a lapsed man, strove with rash audacity to be received by the Church without any loss of grade and honor. When they were frustrated in this attempt, and saw that Felix with Sabinus had been substituted in their place, Basilides, wasting away with envy, betook himself to Rome to the apostolic see; and there complaining that he had been unjustly deprived of his see, he presented a supplication full of many falsehoods to Stephen, the Roman Pontiff, and through it besought to be restored to his see and former honor. When the bishops of Spain heard this, they themselves, the two substituted bishops, Sabinus and Felix, were sent to the African Church, and letters were communicated to both, written to Cyprian and the rest of the bishops of his church, that namely the same bishops might take cognizance of the cause of the two libellatici and judge it. Cyprian, therefore, having received the legates together with the aforesaid letters, convoked his colleague bishops, and in their sacred assembly caused the letters written to him and his colleagues to be read and recited, and the same legates to be brought into the council and their own cause to be narrated. Which things having been done, that which had been constituted by the bishops of Spain concerning the deposition of Basilides and Martialis, the libellatici bishops, and concerning the substitution of Sabinus and Felix, was confirmed by the bishops of the African church who were present at this council; namely, that all libellatici indeed can be admitted to do penance, but are rightly forbidden from clerical ordination and priestly dignity; that these two also had been rightly despoiled of their episcopal dignity, and Sabinus and Felix rightly substituted in their place. This testimony having been had, Sabinus and Felix themselves, fortified by the letters of Cyprian and the other bishops written to Stephen and the bishops of Spain, sailed to Rome, both to refute the falsehoods of Basilides and also to restrain by the authority of the apostolic see the contumacious despisers of the bishops of Spain. See Baronius, year 258, numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.","Hoc concilium habitum est in Africa, anno Christi 258 ad instantiam Hispaniae episcoporum, eo fine, ut de causa Basilidis et Martialis, Africanae ecclesiae judicium cognoscerent. Causa talis erat: Basilides Legionensis et Martialis Asturicensis ecclesiae episcopi, eo quod in persecutione fuissent lapsi, libellatici, qui nimirum privatim fidem abnegantes, publicum idolorum cultum pecunia redimissent, episcopali honore ad denuntiationem fidelium Hispanorum spoliantur, et in eorum locum Sabinus ac Felix substituuntur. Cessante persecutione, illi, sicut antea Felicissimus in Africa lapsus, absque ullo gradus et honoris dispendio ab Ecclesia recipi temerario ausu contenderunt: quo conatu cum frustrarentur, Felicemque cum Sabino in locum suum substitutos viderent, Basilides livore tabescens ad apostolicam sedem Romam se contulit; ibique se injuste sede privatum conquerens, Stephano Romano Pontifici supplicationem plurimis mendaciis refertam exhibet, ac per eam sedi, ac honori pristino restitui suppliciter rogat. Quod cum episcopi Hispaniae audissent, ipsi duo suffecti episcopi, Sabinus ac Felix, ad Africanam Ecclesiam ablegantur, et utrique litterae ad Cyprianum reliquosque ejus ecclesiae episcopos scriptae communicantur, ut scilicet iidem episcopi libellaticorum duorum causam cognoscant ac dijudicent. Cyprianus igitur cum legatos una cum litteris praedictis excepisset, collegas antistites convocat, et in eorum sacro concessu litteras ad se suosque collegas scriptas legi ac recitari, et ab iisdem legatis in concilium introductis suam ipsorum causam enarrari procurat. Quae cum facta essent, hoc quod ab episcopis Hispaniae de Basilidis et Martialis libellaticorum episcoporum depositione, et Sabini cum Felici suffectione constitutum fuerat, per Africanae ecclesiae episcopos illos, qui huic concilio interfuerant, confirmatum est; nimirum libellaticos omnes ad poenitentiam quidem agendam posse admitti, verum ab ordinatione clericali, atque sacerdotali dignitate recte prohiberi: hosce duos etiam sua episcopali dignitate recte spoliatos, Sabinum ac Felicem in locum ipsorum recte suffectos esse. Quo testimonio habito, ipse Sabinus ac Felix, litteris Cypriani aliorumque episcoporum ad Stephanum et episcopos Hispaniae scriptis communiti, Romam adnavigant, tum ut mendacia Basilidis refellant, tum etiam ut auctoritate sedis apostolicae contumaces episcoporum Hispaniae contemptores compescant. Vide Baron., anno 258, num. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p14","Prooemium.","","CYPRIANUS, CAECILIUS, PRIMUS, POLYCARPUS, NICOMEDES, LUCILIANUS, SUCCESSUS, SEDATUS, FORTUNATUS, JANUARIUS, SECUNDINUS, POMPONIUS, HONORATUS, VICTOR, AURELIUS, SATIUS, PETRUS, ALIUS JANUARIUS, SATURNINUS, ALIUS AURELIUS, VENANTIUS, QUIETUS, ROGATIANUS, TENAX, FELIX, FAUSTUS, QUINTUS, ALIUS SATURNINUS, LUCIUS, VINCENTIUS, LIBOSUS, GEMINIUS, MARCELLUS, JAMBUS, ADELPHIUS, VICTORICUS, ET PAULUS, FELICI PRESBYTERO ET PLEBIBUS CONSISTENTIBUS AD LEGIONEM, ET ASTURICAE ITEM LAELIO DIACONO ET PLEBI EMERITAE CONSISTENTIBUS FRATRIBUS IN DOMINO SALUTEM."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p15","Prooemium.","Since we had come together into one, we read your letters, most beloved brothers, which you sent to us through Felix and Sabinus, our fellow bishops, for the integrity of your faith and for the fear of God, signifying that Basilides and Martialis, stained with the libelli of idolatry and bound by the consciousness of nefarious deeds, ought not to bear the episcopate and administer the priesthood of God; and you desired that a response be written to you concerning these things, and that your just and equally necessary solicitude be relieved by the solace or help of our sentence. But to this your desire not so much our counsels as the divine precepts respond, which have long ago been commanded by heavenly voice and prescribed by God's law, who and what sort of men ought to serve at the altar and celebrate divine sacrifices. For in Exodus God speaks to Moses and warns, saying: \"The priests who approach the Lord God, let them be sanctified, lest the Lord forsake them\" (Ex 19:22). And again: \"And when they approach to minister at the holy altar, let them not bring iniquity upon themselves, lest they die\" (Ex 28:43). Likewise in Leviticus the Lord commands and says: \"A man in whom there is a blemish or a defect shall not approach to offer gifts to God\" (Lev 21:17).","CYPRIAN, CAECILIUS, PRIMUS, POLYCARPUS, NICOMEDES, LUCILIANUS, SUCCESSUS, SEDATUS, FORTUNATUS, JANUARIUS, SECUNDINUS, POMPONIUS, HONORATUS, VICTOR, AURELIUS, SATIUS, PETRUS, ANOTHER JANUARIUS, SATURNINUS, ANOTHER AURELIUS, VENANTIUS, QUIETUS, ROGATIANUS, TENAX, FELIX, FAUSTUS, QUINTUS, ANOTHER SATURNINUS, LUCIUS, VINCENTIUS, LIBOSUS, GEMINIUS, MARCELLUS, JAMBUS, ADELPHIUS, VICTORICUS, AND PAULUS, TO FELIX, PRESBYTER, AND TO THE PEOPLES RESIDING AT LEGIO AND ASTURICA, AND ALSO TO LAELIUS, DEACON, AND TO THE PEOPLE RESIDING AT EMERITA, BROTHERS, GREETINGS IN THE LORD."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p16","I.","Which things, since they are foretold and manifest to us, it is necessary that our obediences serve the divine precepts; nor can human indulgence accept person in such matters, or bestow anything on anyone, where divine prescription intervenes and imposes law. Nor should we be unmindful of what the Lord spoke to the Jews through Isaiah the prophet, rebuking and indignant that, despising divine precepts, they followed human doctrines. \"This people,\" He says, \"honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me. But in vain do they worship me, teaching the commandments and doctrines of men\" (Is 29:13). Which thing the Lord also repeats in the Gospel and says: \"You reject the commandment of God that you may establish your own tradition\" (Mk 7:13). Having these things before our eyes and considering them solicitously and religiously, in the ordinations of priests we ought to choose only immaculate and whole prelates, who, offering sacrifices to God holily and worthily, can be heard in the prayers which they make for the safety of the Lord's people, since it is written: \"God does not hear the sinner; but if any man is a worshipper of God and does his will, him He hears\" (Jn 9:31). Wherefore, with full diligence and sincere scrutiny, those ought to be chosen for the priesthood of God who are known to be heard by God.","Cum in unum convenissemus, legimus litteras vestras, fratres dilectissimi, quas ad nos per Felicem et Sabinum coepiscopos nostros pro fidei vestrae integritate et pro Dei timore fecistis, significantes Basilidem et Martialem libellis idololatriae commaculatos et nefandorum facinorum conscientia vinctos episcopatum gerere et sacerdotium Dei administrare non oportere; et desiderastis rescribi ad haec vobis, et justam pariter ac necessariam sollicitudinem vestram vel solatio vel auxilio nostrae sententiae sublevari. Sed enim desiderio huic vestro non tam nostra consilia quam divina praecepta respondent, quibus jampridem mandatur voce coelesti, et Dei lege praescribitur quos et quales oporteat deservire altari et sacrificia divina celebrare. In Exodo namque ad Moysem Deus loquitur et monet dicens: Sacerdotes qui accedunt ad Dominum Deum sanctificentur, ne forte derelinquat illos Dominus. Et iterum: Et cum accedunt ministrare ad altare sancti, non adducent in se delictum, ne moriantur. Item in Levitico praecipit Dominus et dicit: Homo in quo fuerit macula et vitium non accedet offerre dona Deo."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p17","II.","Nor should the people flatter themselves as if they can be immune from the contagion of the offense when communicating with a sinning priest and lending their consent to the unjust and illicit episcopate of their prelate, since through the prophet Hosea the divine censure threatens and says: \"Their sacrifices are like the bread of mourning; all who eat them shall be defiled\" (Hos 9:4), teaching and showing that all without exception are bound to sin who have been defiled by the sacrifice of a profane and unjust priest. How many also we find manifested in Numbers, when Chore, Dathan, and Abiram claimed for themselves license to sacrifice against Aaron the priest. There also the Lord commanded through Moses that the people be separated from them, lest being joined with the evildoers he himself be also bound by the same crime. \"Separate yourselves,\" He says, \"from the tabernacles of these most hard men, and touch nothing that belongs to them, lest you perish together in their sin\" (Num 16:26). Wherefore the people, obeying the Lord's precepts and fearing God, ought to separate themselves from a sinning prelate, and not mix themselves with the sacrifices of a sacrilegious priest, since they themselves have the power either to elect worthy priests or to refuse the unworthy.","Quae cum praedicta et manifesta sint nobis, praeceptis divinis necesse est obsequia nostra deserviant; nec personam in ejusmodi rebus accipere aut aliquid cuiquam largiri potest humana indulgentia ubi intercedit et legem tribuit divina praescriptio. Neque enim immemores esse debemus quid ad Judaeos per Esaiam prophetam locutus sit Dominus increpans et indignans quod, contemptis divinis praeceptis, humanas doctrinas sequerentur. Populus iste, inquit, labiis suis honorificat me, cor vero eorum longe separatum est a me. Sine causa autem colunt me, mandata et doctrinas hominum docentes. Quod item Dominus in Evangelio repetit et dicit: Rejicitis mandatum Dei ut traditionem vestram statuatis. Quae ante oculos habentes et sollicite ac religiose considerantes, in ordinationibus sacerdotum non nisi immaculatos et integros antistites eligere debemus, qui sancte et digne sacrificia Deo offerentes audiri in precibus possint quas faciunt pro plebis dominicae incolumitate, cum scriptum sit: Deus peccatorem non audit; sed si quis Deum coluerit et voluntatem ejus fecerit, illum audit. Propter quod plena diligentia et exploratione sincera oportet eos ad sacerdotium Dei deligi quos a Deo constet audiri."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p18","III.","Which very thing we see descending from divine authority, that a priest be chosen in the presence of the people under the eyes of all, and be approved as worthy and suitable by public judgment and testimony, just as in Numbers the Lord commanded Moses, saying: \"Take Aaron your brother and Eleazar his son, and place them on the mountain before the whole synagogue, and strip Aaron of his garment and put it on Eleazar his son, and let Aaron be gathered and die there\" (Num 20:25-26). God commands a priest to be constituted before the whole synagogue, that is, He instructs and shows that ordinations of priests ought to be done only with the conscience of the assisting people, that in the presence of the people the crimes of the wicked may be disclosed or the merits of the good proclaimed, and the ordination be just and legitimate which has been examined by the suffrage and judgment of all. Which afterwards, according to the divine teaching, is observed in the Acts of the Apostles, when concerning ordaining one in place of Judas, Peter speaks to the people. \"Peter rose up,\" he says, \"in the midst of the disciples; and there was a crowd together of about one hundred and twenty\" (Acts 1:15). And we observe that the apostles observed this not only in the ordinations of bishops and priests, but also in those of deacons, concerning which also it is written in their acts: \"And the twelve called together the whole multitude of the disciples and said to them\" (Acts 6:2). Which was done so diligently and cautiously with the whole people convoked for this reason, lest any unworthy person should creep into the ministry of the altar or into the priestly place. For that sometimes the unworthy are ordained not according to the will of God but according to human presumption, and that this displeases God which does not come from legitimate and just ordination, God Himself manifests through the prophet Hosea, saying: \"They have set up kings for themselves, and not through me\" (Hos 8:4).","Nec sibi plebs blandiatur quasi immunis esse a contagio delicti possit cum sacerdote peccatore communicans et ad injustum atque illicitum praepositi sui episcopatum consensum suum commodans, quando per Osee prophetam comminetur et dicat censura divina: Sacrificia eorum tamquam panis luctus, omnes qui manducant ea contaminabuntur, docens scilicet et ostendens omnes omnino ad peccatum constringi qui fuerint profani et injusti sacerdotis sacrificio contaminati. Quot item in Numeris manifestari invenimus, quando Chore et Dathan et Abiron contra Aaron sacerdotem sacrificandi sibi licentiam vindicaverunt. Illic quoque per Moysem praecepit Dominus ut ab eis populus separetur, ne facinorosis conjunctus eodem facinore et ipse perstringatur. Separamini, inquit, a tabernaculis horum hominum durissimorum, et nolite tangere ea quae ad eos pertinent, ne simul pereatis in peccato eorum. Propter quod plebs obsequens praeceptis dominicis et Deum metuens a peccatore praeposito separare se debet, nec se ad sacrilegi sacerdotis sacrificia miscere, quando ipsa maxime habeat potestatem vel eligendi dignos sacerdotes vel indignos recusandi."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p19","IV.","Wherefore, what is to be diligently preserved from divine and apostolic tradition and observation, and what is also held among us and almost through all provinces, that for ordinations to be duly celebrated, the nearest bishops of that province to which the prelate is ordained should come together, and the bishop be chosen in the presence of the people, who most fully know the life of each and have perceived the act of each from his conversation. Which we see was also done among you in the ordination of our colleague Sabinus, that the episcopate was conferred upon him by the suffrage of the whole brotherhood and by the judgment of the bishops who had come together in the present and who had made letters concerning him to you, and hands were imposed upon him in the place of Basilides. Nor can Basilides, after his crimes have been detected and his conscience laid bare even by his own confession, rescind an ordination perfectly justly accomplished, by going to Rome and deceiving our colleague Stephen, placed far away and ignorant of the deed and the truth, that he might obtain by canvassing to be unjustly restored to the episcopate from which he had been justly deposed. Which tends to this, that the crimes of Basilides have not so much been abolished as heaped up, so that to his former sins the crime also of deceit and circumvention has been added. For he who has been negligently circumvented is not so much to be blamed as he is to be execrated who has fraudulently circumvented. But if Basilides could circumvent men, he cannot circumvent God, since it is written: \"God is not mocked\" (Gal 6:7). But neither can deceit profit Martialis, so that he also, involved in grave crimes, should not hold the episcopate, since the Apostle also warns and says: \"A bishop ought to be without crime, as a steward of God\" (Tit 1:7).","Quod et ipsum videmus de divina auctoritate descendere, ut sacerdos plebe praesente sub omnium oculis deligatur et dignus atque idoneus publico judicio ac testimonio comprobetur, sicut in Numeris Dominus Moysi praecepit dicens: Apprehende Aaron fratrem tuum et Eleazarum filium ejus, et imponens eos in montem coram omni synagoga, et exue Aaron stolam ejus, et indue Eleazarum filium ejus, et Aaron appositus moriatur illic. Coram omni synagoga jubet Deus constitui sacerdotem, id est, instruit et ostendit ordinationes sacerdotales non nisi sub populi assistentis conscientia fieri oportere, ut plebe praesente vel detegantur malorum crimina vel bonorum merita praedicentur, et sit ordinatio justa et legitima quae omnium suffragio et judicio fuerit examinata. Quod postea secundum divina magisteria observatur in Actis apostolorum, quando de ordinando in locum Judae apostolo Petrus ad plebem loquitur. Surrexit, inquit, Petrus in medio discentium, fuit autem turba in uno. Nec hoc in episcoporum tantum et sacerdotum, sed et in diaconorum ordinationibus observasse apostolos animadvertimus, de quo et ipso in actis eorum scriptum est: Et convocaverunt, inquit, illi duodecim totam plebem discipulorum et dixerunt eis. Quod utique idcirco tam diligenter et caute convocata plebe tota gerebatur, ne quis ad altaris ministerium vel ad sacerdotalem locum indignus obreperet. Ordinari enim nonnumquam indignos non secundum Dei voluntatem, sed secundum humanam praesumptionem, et haec Deo displicere quae non veniant ex legitima et justa ordinatione, Deus ipse manifestat per Osee prophetam dicens: Sibimetipsis constituerunt regem, et non per me."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p20","V.","Wherefore, since, as you have written, most beloved brothers, and as Felix and Sabinus our colleagues assert, and as the other Felix of Caesaraugusta, a cultivator and defender of the truth, signifies in his letters, Basilides and Martialis are stained with the nefarious libellus of idolatry; Basilides moreover, besides the stain of the libellus, when he lay in infirmity, blasphemed against God and confessed that he had blasphemed, and voluntarily laying down the episcopate on account of the wound of his conscience, turned to do penance, praying to God, and sufficiently gratified if he were permitted to communicate even as a layman; Martialis also, besides the base and filthy banquets of gentiles long frequented in the college, and children placed in the same college after the manner of foreign nations at profane sepulchers and buried with aliens, also in the acts held publicly before the procurator ducenarius, attested that he had obeyed idolatry and denied Christ; and since there are many and grave other crimes by which Basilides and Martialis are held implicated, such men vainly strive to usurp the episcopate for themselves, since it is manifest that such men neither can preside over the Church of Christ nor ought to offer sacrifices to God; especially since our colleague Cornelius also, a peaceful and just priest and also honored by the Lord with martyrdom, long ago with us and with absolutely all bishops constituted throughout the whole world decreed that such men can indeed be admitted to do penance, but are to be prohibited from ordination of the clergy and priestly honor.","Propter quod diligenter de traditione divina et apostolica observatione servandum est et tenendum quod apud nos quoque et fere per provincias universas tenetur, ut ad ordinationes rite celebrandas ad eam plebem cui praepositus ordinatur, episcopi ejusdem provinciae proximi quique conveniant, et episcopus deligatur plebe praesente, quae singulorum vitam plenissime novit et uniuscujusque actum de ejus conversatione perspexit. Quod et apud vos factum videmus in Sabini collegae nostri ordinatione, ut de universae fraternitatis suffragio et de episcoporum qui in praesentia convenerant, quique de eo ad vos litteras fecerant, judicio episcopatus ei deferretur et manus ei in locum Basilidis imponeretur. Nec rescindere ordinationem jure perfectam potest quod Basilides post crimina sua detecta et conscientiam etiam propria confessione nudatam Romam pergens Stephanum collegam nostrum longe positum et gestae rei ac veritatis ignarum fefellit, ut exambiret reponi se injuste in episcopatum de quo fuerat jure depositus. Hoc eo pertinet ut Basilidis non tam abolita sint quam cumulata delicta, ut ad superiora peccata ejus etiam fallaciae et circumventionis crimen accesserit. Neque enim tam culpandus est ille cui negligenter obreptum est quam hic execrandus qui fraudulenter obrepsit. Obrepere autem si hominibus Basilides potuit, Deo non potest, cum scriptum sit: Deus non deridetur. Sed nec Martiali potuit profuisse fallacia quo minus ipse quoque delictis gravibus involutus episcopatum tenere non debeat, quando et Apostolus moneat et dicat: Episcopum oportet esse sine crimine quasi Dei dispensatorem."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p21","VI.","Nor let it move you, most beloved brothers, if among some in these last times either slippery faith wavers, or the fear of God is irreligious, or peaceful concord does not persevere. These things have been foretold as about to happen at the end of the world, and by the voice of the Lord and the testimony of the Apostles it has been predicted that, with the world now failing and Antichrist approaching, all good things will fail, but evil and adverse things will increase. Not, however, in these last times has the evangelical vigor fallen, nor has the strength of Christian virtue or the power of faith languished, so that there does not remain a portion of priests who by no means succumb to these ruins of things and shipwrecks of faith, but keep the honor of divine majesty and priestly dignity with full observance of fear. We remember and hold that, while others succumb and yield, Mathathias (1 Macc 2:19-20) strongly vindicated the law of God; Elijah (1 Kings 19:10), while the Jews failed and departed from divine religion, stood and fought sublimely; Daniel (Dan 6:10, 21-22), neither deterred by the solitude of a foreign region nor by the frequent infestation of persecution, gave martyrdoms frequently and strongly; the three boys, neither broken by years nor by threats, faithfully withstood the Babylonian fires and conquered the victorious king in his very captivity. Let the number of prevaricators or betrayers, who now in the Church rise up against the Church and begin to undermine faith and truth equally, look to it. There remains among many a sincere mind and whole religion, and a soul devoted to none other than its Lord and God, nor does foreign perfidy depress Christian faith to ruin, but rather excites and exalts it to glory, according to what the blessed Apostle Paul exhorts and says: \"What if some of them have fallen from faith? Shall their unbelief make the faith of God without effect? God forbid. For God is true, but every man a liar\" (Rom 3:3-4). But if every man is a liar and God alone is true, what else ought we, the servants and especially the priests of God, to do, but to leave human errors and falsehoods, and, keeping the Lord's precepts, to remain in the truth of God?","Quapropter cum, sicut scripsistis, fratres dilectissimi, et ut Felix et Sabinus collegae nostri asseverant, utque alius Felix de Caesaraugusta fidei cultor ac defensor veritatis litteris suis significat, Basilides et Martialis nefando idololatriae libello contaminati sint, Basilides adhuc insuper, praeter libelli maculam, cum in infirmitate decumberet, in Deum blasphemaverit et se blasphemasse confessus sit, et episcopatum pro conscientiae suae vulnere sponte deponens ad agendam poenitentiam conversus sit, Deum deprecans, et satis gratulans si sibi vel laico communicare contingeret. Martialis quoque praeter gentilium turpia et lutulenta convivia in collegio diu frequentata et filios in eodem collegio exterarum gentium more apud profana sepulchra depositos et alienigenis consepultos, actis etiam publice habitis apud procuratorem ducenarium obtemperasse se idololatriae et Christum negasse contestatus sit, cumque alia multa sint et gravia delicta quibus Basilides et Martialis implicati tenentur, frustra tales episcopatum sibi usurpare conantur, cum manifestum sit ejusmodi homines nec Ecclesiae Christi posse praeesse nec Deo sacrificia offerre debere; maxime cum jampridem nobiscum et cum omnibus omnino episcopis in toto mundo constitutis etiam Cornelius collega noster sacerdos pacificus ac justus, et martyrio quoque dignatione Domini honoratus, decreverit ejusmodi homines ad poenitentiam quidem agendam posse admitti, ab ordinatione autem cleri atque sacerdotali honore prohiberi."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p22","VII.","Wherefore, although some of our colleagues, most beloved brothers, have existed who think that divine discipline is to be neglected and who rashly communicate with Basilides and Martialis, this thing ought not to disturb our faith, since the Holy Spirit threatens such in the psalms, saying: \"But you have hated discipline and have cast my words behind you. If you saw a thief, you ran with him, and with adulterers you placed your portion\" (Ps 49:17-18). He shows that those become consorts and partakers of the crimes of others who are joined with criminals. But also the Apostle Paul writes and says: \"Whisperers, detractors, hateful to God, injurious, proud, boasters, inventors of evil things; who, having known the justice of God, did not understand that those who do such things are worthy of death, not only they who do them, but also they who consent to those who do them\" (Rom 1:30-32). He manifests and proves that those are worthy of death and to come into punishment, not only those who do evil, but also those who consent to those doing them, who, while they mix themselves with the wicked and sinners and those not doing penance in illicit communication, are polluted by the contracts of the guilty, and while they are joined in fault, so they are not separated in punishment. Wherefore, most beloved brothers, we both praise and approve the religious solicitude of your integrity and faith, and as much as we can we exhort by our letters, that you not mix yourselves with profane and stained priests in sacrilegious communication, but keep the whole and sincere firmness of your faith with religious fear. I wish you, most dear brothers, always to be well.","Nec vos moveat, fratres dilectissimi, si apud quosdam in novissimis temporibus aut lubrica fides nutat, aut Dei timor irreligiosus vacillat aut pacifica concordia non perseverat. Praenuntiata sunt haec futura in saeculi fine, et Domini voce atque Apostolorum contestatione praedictum est deficiente jam mundo, atque appropinquante antichristo, bona quaeque deficere, mala vero et adversa proficere. Non sic tamen, quamvis novissimis temporibus, in Ecclesia Dei aut evangelicus vigor cecidit, aut Christianae virtutis aut fidei robur elanguit, ut non supersit portio sacerdotum quae minime ad has rerum ruinas et fidei naufragia succumbat, sed fortis et stabilis honorem divinae majestatis et sacerdotalem dignitatem plena timoris observatione tueatur. Meminimus et tenemus, succumbentibus licet et cedentibus caeteris Mathathiam legem Dei vindicasse fortiter. Heliam, Judaeis deficientibus atque a religione divina recedentibus, stetisse et certasse sublimiter; Danielem nec solitudine regionis alienae nec persecutionis assiduae infestatione deterritum frequenter ac fortiter gloriosa dedisse martyria; tres item pueros nec annis nec minis fractos contra ignes Babylonios fideliter obstitisse et victorem regem in ipsa sua captivitate vicisse. Viderit vel praevaricatorum numerus vel proditorum qui nunc in Ecclesia contra Ecclesiam surgere et fidem pariter ac veritatem labefactare coeperunt. Permanet apud plurimos sincera mens et religio integra, et non nisi Domino et Deo suo anima devota, nec christianam fidem aliena perfidia deprimit ad ruinam, sed magis excitat et exaltat ad gloriam, secundum quod beatus apostolus Paulus hortatur et dicit: Quid enim si exciderunt a fide quidam eorum, numquid infidelitas illorum fidem Dei evacuavit? Absit. Est enim Deus verax, omnis autem homo mendax. Si autem omnis homo mendax est et solus Deus verax, quid aliud servi, et maxime sacerdotes Dei, facere debemus nisi ut humanos errores et mendacia relinquamus, et praecepta dominica custodientes in Dei veritate maneamus?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p23","VIII.","","Quare, etsi aliqui de collegis nostris extiterint, fratres dilectissimi, qui deificam disciplinam negligendam putant et cum Basilide et Martiale temere communicant, conturbare fidem nostram res ista non debet, cum Spiritus sanctus in psalmis talibus comminetur dicens: Tu autem odisti disciplinam, et abjecisti sermones meos retro. Si videbas furem, concurrebas ei, et cum adulteris portionem tuam ponebas. Consortes et participes ostendit eos alienorum delictorum fieri qui fuerint delinquentibus copulati. Sed et hoc idem Paulus apostolus scribit et dicit: Susurratores, detractores, abhorrentes Deo, injuriosi, superbi, jactantes sui, adinventores malorum, qui cum justitiam Dei cognovissent, non intellexerunt quoniam qui talia agunt morte sunt digni, non tantum qui faciunt ea, sed et qui consentiunt eis qui haec agunt: quoniam qui talia, inquit, agunt, morte sunt digni. Manifestat et comprobat morte dignos esse et ad poenam venire, non tantum illos qui mala faciunt, sed etiam eos qui talia agentibus consentiunt, qui dum malis et peccatoribus et poenitentiam non agentibus illicita communicatione miscentur, nocentium contractibus polluuntur, et dum junguntur in culpa, sic nec in poena separantur. Propter quod integritatis et fidei vestrae religiosam sollicitudinem, fratres dilectissimi, et laudamus pariter et probamus; et quantum possumus adhortamur litteris nostris, ne vos cum profanis et maculatis sacerdotibus communicatione sacrilega misceatis, sed integram et sinceram fidei vestrae firmitatem religioso timore servetis. Opto vos, fratres charissimi, semper bene valere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p24","VIII.","IN THE YEAR OF CHRIST 256, IN THE PONTIFICATE OF STEPHEN III. CARTHAGINIAN COUNCIL UNDER CYPRIAN THE FIFTH, FIRST ON BAPTISM.","ANNO CHRISTI CCLVI, PONTIFICATUS S. STEPHANI III. CARTHAGINENSE CONCILIUM SUB CYPRIANO QUINTUM, DE BAPTISMO PRIMUM."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p25","Prooemium.","After in the African church, with Agrippinus or even Tertullian as author, the custom of rebaptizing those who had been baptized by heretics had long before been frequently used, and Novatian, that at least in this way he might receive the lapsed, whom he taught to be unclean, washed by baptism from all their uncleanness into the Church, had imitated it; Magnus, a layman, on the occasion of the Novatians, who at that time observed the same form and rite of baptizing with the Catholics, first doubted whether those returning to the Church ought to be rebaptized, whom they knew to have been baptized by Novatians. Among the bishops, the first are found to be the Numidians, who wrote letters to Cyprian and other bishops, summoned to this synod at Carthage, concerning repeating the baptism of heretics. This first consultation on this synodical matter, held in the year 258, the 4th of Valerian and Gallienus, is shown by Cyprian's Epistle 73 to Jubaianus, in which, while among the Africans some, according to apostolic tradition, thought that those who had been legitimately baptized by heretics ought not to be rebaptized, others on the contrary thought that baptism is one only of the one Church, and therefore that true and catholic baptism cannot exist among heretics who are outside the Church; this inquiry having preceded, it was finally defined, since there is one Baptism in the holy Church, that no one can be baptized outside the Church; and those who misinterpreted the tradition of the Apostles, and also spoke badly, universally constraining, added, as is established from Cyprian's Epistle 71 to Quintus, that those who had fallen away from the Catholics to heretics, being rightly baptized, should be received not through baptism but through the imposition of hands alone, if they strive to return to the Church. Baronius, year of Christ 258, numbers 17 to 26 inclusive. All these things having been so constituted, they signified to Januarius and the other bishops of Numidia the sentence and definition of this synod, as they themselves had sought by letters written to the synod, by this synodal epistle which we have subjoined here from Cyprian.","Postquam in Africana ecclesia Agrippino, vel etiam Tertulliano auctore, usus rebaptizandi eos qui ab haereticis baptizati erant, jam diu antehac frequentatus fuisset, eamque Novatianus, ut saltem hoc modo lapsos, quos immundos esse docebat, per Baptismum ab omni sua immunditia ablutos in Ecclesiam reciperet, aemulatus fuisset; Magnus, homo laicus, occasione Novatianorum, eamdem tunc temporis baptizandi formam ac ritum cum Catholicis observantium, dubitavit primus, an ad Ecclesiam revertentes rebaptizari deberent, quos a Novatianis baptizatos esse scirent. Ex episcopis primi reperiuntur fuisse Numidae, qui de iterando haereticorum Baptismate ad Cyprianum aliosque episcopos, ad synodum hanc Carthagine coactos, litteras conscripserunt. Prima igitur synodica hac de re consultatio, anno 258, Valeriani et Gallieni imperatorum 4, habita haec est, ut Cypriani Epistola 73. ad Jubaianum ostendit, in qua cum ex Africanis alii, juxta Apostolicam traditionem sentirent non esse rebaptizandos eos qui legitime ab haereticis baptizati sunt: alii vero contra existimarent, unum Baptismum dumtaxat esse unius Ecclesiae, ideoque apud haereticos qui extra Ecclesiam sunt, Baptismum verum et catholicum esse non posse; hac inquisitione praevia, tandem definitum est, cum unum sit in sancta Ecclesia Baptisma, neminem foris extra Ecclesiam baptizari posse: traditionem Apostolorum male interpretantes, male etiam loquentes universaliter constringentes, addiderunt, ut ex epistola 71 Cypriani ad Quintum constat, illos qui a Catholicis rite baptizati ad haereticos defecissent, non per Baptismum, sed per solam manuum impositionem recipiendos esse, si ad Ecclesiam redire contendant. Baron. anno Christi 258, num. 17 usque ad 26 inclusive. Quibus omnibus ita constitutis, Januario caeterisque Numidiae episcopis sententiam ac definitionem hujus synodi, prout ipsi per litteras ad synodum scriptas expetiverant, hac synodica epistola, quam infra ex Cypriano hic subjecimus, significarunt."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p26","Prooemium.","CYPRIANUS, LIBERALIS, CALDONIUS, JUNIUS, PRIMUS, CAECILIUS, POLYCARPUS, NICOMEDES, FELIX, MARRUTIUS, SUCCESSUS, LUCIANUS, HONORATUS, FORTUNATUS, VICTOR, DONATUS, LUCIUS, HERCULANUS, POMPONIUS, DEMETRIUS, QUINTUS, SATURNINUS, MARCUS, ALIUS SATURNINUS, ALIUS DONATUS, ROGATIANUS, SEDATUS, TERTULLUS, HORTENSIANUS, ITEM ALIUS SATURNINUS, SATTIUS, JANUARIO, SATURNINO, MAXIMO, VICTORI, ALIO VICTORI, CASSIO, PROCULO, MODIANO, CITTINO, GARGILIO, EUTYCIANO, ALIO GARGILIO, ALIO SATURNINO, NEMESIANO, NAMPULO, ANTONIANO, ROGATIANO, HONORATO FRATRIBUS SALUTEM.","CYPRIAN, LIBERALIS, CALDONIUS, JUNIUS, PRIMUS, CAECILIUS, POLYCARPUS, NICOMEDES, FELIX, MARRUTIUS, SUCCESSUS, LUCIANUS, HONORATUS, FORTUNATUS, VICTOR, DONATUS, LUCIUS, HERCULANUS, POMPONIUS, DEMETRIUS, QUINTUS, SATURNINUS, MARCUS, ANOTHER SATURNINUS, ANOTHER DONATUS, ROGATIANUS, SEDATUS, TERTULLUS, HORTENSIANUS, ALSO ANOTHER SATURNINUS, SATTIUS, TO JANUARIUS, SATURNINUS, MAXIMUS, VICTOR, ANOTHER VICTOR, CASSIUS, PROCULUS, MODIANUS, CITTINUS, GARGILIUS, EUTYCIANUS, ANOTHER GARGILIUS, ANOTHER SATURNINUS, NEMESIANUS, NAMPULUS, ANTONIANUS, ROGATIANUS, HONORATUS, BROTHERS, GREETINGS."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p27","I.","When we were together in council, most dear brothers, we read your letters which you made to us concerning those who are seen to have been baptized among heretics and schismatics, whether when they come to the catholic Church, which is one and true, they ought to be baptized. Concerning which matter, although you yourselves there hold the truth and firmness of the catholic rule, yet because you thought we should be consulted for common love, we set forth our sentence, not new, but long ago established by our predecessors and observed by us, joining with you in equal consensus, judging and holding for certain that no one can be baptized outside the Church, since there is one Baptism established in the holy Church, and it is written, the Lord saying: \"They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and have dug for themselves broken cisterns that can hold no water\" (Jer 2:13). And again the divine Scripture warns and says: \"Abstain from alien water, and do not drink from a foreign fountain\" (Prov 9:18, LXX). Therefore the water must first be cleansed and sanctified by the priest, that it may be able by its baptism to wash away the sins of the man who is baptized, because through Ezekiel the prophet the Lord says: \"I will sprinkle clean water upon you, and you shall be cleansed from all your uncleannesses, and from all your idols I will cleanse you, and I will give you a new heart and a new spirit in you\" (Ezek 36:25-26). But how can he cleanse and sanctify water who himself is unclean and in whom the Holy Spirit is not, since the Lord says in Numbers: \"Everything that the unclean touches shall be unclean\" (Num 19:22). Or how can a baptizer give to another remission of sins, who himself cannot lay aside his own sins outside the Church?","Cum simul in concilio essemus, fratres charissimi, legimus litteras vestras quas ad nos fecistis de iis qui apud haereticos et schismaticos baptizati videntur, an ad Ecclesiam catholicam, quae una et vera est, venientes baptizari debeant. De qua re quamquam et ipsi illic veritatem et firmitatem catholicae regulae teneatis, tamen quoniam consulendos nos pro communi dilectione existimastis, sententiam nostram non novam promimus, sed jampridem ab antecessoribus nostris statutam et a nobis observatam vobiscum pari consensione conjungimus, censentes scilicet et pro certo tenentes neminem foris baptizari extra Ecclesiam posse, cum sit Baptisma unum in sancta Ecclesia constitutum, et scriptum sit, Domino dicente: Me derelinquerunt fontem aquae vivae, et effoderunt sibi lacus detritos qui non possunt aquam portare. Et iterum divina Scriptura moneat et dicat: Ab aqua aliena abstinete, et fonte aquae alienae ne biberis. Oportet ergo mundari et sanctificari aquam prius a sacerdote, ut possit Baptismo suo peccata hominis qui baptizatur abluere, quia per Ezechielem prophetam Dominus dicit: Et aspergam super vos aquam mundam, et mundabimini ab omnibus immunditiis vestris, et ab omnibus simulacris vestris emundabo vos, et dabo vobis cor novum et spiritum novum dabo in vobis. Quomodo autem mundare et sanctificare aquam potest qui ipse immundus est et apud quem Spiritus sanctus non est cum Dominus dicat in Numeris: Et omnia quaecumque tetigerit immundus immunda erant. Aut quomodo baptizans dare alteri remissam peccatorum potest qui ipse sua peccata deponere extra Ecclesiam non potest?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p28","II.","But the very interrogation which is made in baptism is a witness of the truth. For when we say: \"Do you believe in eternal life and remission of sins through the holy Church?\" we understand that remission of sins is given only in the Church, but among heretics, where the Church is not, sins cannot be forgiven. Therefore those who assert that heretics can baptize must either change the interrogation or vindicate the truth, unless they also attribute to them the Church, whose baptism they contend they have. He who has been baptized must also be anointed, that having received the chrism, that is, the Anointing, he may be the anointed of God and may have the grace of Christ in him. Moreover, the Eucharist is that from which the baptized are anointed with the oil sanctified on the altar. But he could not sanctify the creation of oil who had neither an altar nor a Church. Hence neither can spiritual anointing exist among heretics, since it is established that oil cannot be sanctified nor the Eucharist be made among them at all. But we ought to know and remember that it is written: \"Let not the oil of a sinner anoint my head\" (Ps 140:5), which the Holy Spirit before warned in the psalms, lest any, going astray and wandering from the way of truth, should be anointed among heretics and adversaries of Christ. But for the baptized, what prayer can a sacrilegious and sinful priest make, since it is written: \"God does not hear the sinner; but he who worships him and does his will, him he hears\" (Jn 9:31). But who can give what he does not have, or how can he perform spiritual things who himself has lost the Holy Spirit? And therefore he who comes raw to the Church must be baptized and renewed, that he may be sanctified within by the holy, because it is written: \"Be holy, because I am holy, says the Lord\" (Lev 19:2; 20:26); that he who has been seduced into error and washed outside in the true and ecclesiastical baptism may lay down this very thing, that coming to God while seeking a priest, he incurred a sacrilegious man by the fraud of error.","Sed et ipsa interrogatio quae fit in Baptismo testis est veritatis. Nam cum dicimus: Credis in vitam aeternam et remissionem peccatorum per sanctam Ecclesiam? intelligimus remissionem peccatorum nonnisi in Ecclesia dari, apud haereticos autem, ubi Ecclesia non sit, non posse peccata dimitti. Itaque qui haereticos baptizare posse asserunt, aut interrogationem mutent, aut vindicent veritatem, nisi si eis et Ecclesiam tribuunt quos Baptisma habere contendunt. Ungi quoque necesse est eum qui baptizatus sit, ut accepto Chrismate, id est, Unctione, esse unctus Dei et habere in se gratiam Christi possit. Porro autem Eucharistia est unde baptizati unguntur oleo in altari sanctificato. Sanctificare autem non potuit olei creaturam qui nec altare habuit nec Ecclesiam. Unde nec unctio spiritalis apud haereticos potest esse, quando constet oleum sanctificari et Eucharistiam fieri apud illos omnino non posse. Scire autem et meminisse debemus scriptum esse: Oleum peccatoris non ungat caput meum, quod ante in psalmis praemonuit Spiritus sanctus, ne quis exorbitans et a via veritatis exerrans apud haereticos et Christi adversarios ungeretur. Sed et pro baptizato quam precem facere potest sacerdos sacrilegus et peccator, cum scriptum sit: Deus peccatorem non audit; sed qui eum coluerit, et voluntatem ejus fecerit, illum audit. Quis autem potest dare quod ipse non habeat, aut quomodo potest spiritalia agere qui ipse amiserit Spiritum sanctum? Et idcirco baptizandus est et innovandus qui ad Ecclesiam rudis venit, ut intus per Sanctos sanctificetur, quia scriptum est: Sancti estote, quia et ego sanctus sum, dicit Dominus: ut qui in errorem seductus est et foris tinctus in Baptismo vero et ecclesiastico etiam hoc ipsum deponat quod homo ad Deum veniens, dum sacerdotem quaerit, in sacrilegum fraude erroris incurrit."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p29","III.","Moreover, to approve the baptism of heretics and schismatics is to consent to that which they have baptized. For that part cannot be void there and the other part prevail. If he could baptize, he could also give the Holy Spirit. But if he cannot give the Holy Spirit, because, being constituted outside, he is not with the Holy Spirit, he cannot baptize the one coming, since both Baptism is one, and the Holy Spirit is one, and one Church founded by Christ the Lord upon Peter, from the origin of unity and by reason of foundation. Thus it happens that since all things among them are void and false, nothing of those things which they have done ought to be approved by us. For what can be valid and firm with God which they do, whom the Lord calls His enemies and adversaries in His Gospel, saying: \"He who is not with me is against me; and he who does not gather with me scatters\" (Lk 11:23). And also the blessed Apostle John, keeping the Lord's commands and precepts, put in his epistle, saying: \"You have heard that Antichrist is coming. Now many antichrists have come. Hence we know that it is the last hour. They went out from us, but they were not of us. For if they had been of us, they would have remained with us\" (1 Jn 2:18-19). Hence we also ought to gather and consider whether those who are adversaries of the Lord and have been called antichrists can give the grace of Christ. Therefore we who are with the Lord and hold the unity of the Lord and administer His priesthood in the Church according to His condescension, ought to repudiate and reject and hold as profane whatever the adversaries of the Lord and antichrists do, and to give to those who, coming from error and perversity, acknowledge the true faith of the one Church, all the divine sacraments of grace and the truth of unity and faith. We wish you, most dear brothers, always to be well.","Caeterum probare est haereticorum et schismaticorum baptisma consentire in id quod illi baptizaverint. Neque enim potest pars illic inanis esse et pars praevalere. Si Baptizare potuit, potuit et Spiritum sanctum dare. Si autem Spiritum sanctum dare non potest, quia foris constitutus cum sancto Spiritu non est, nec baptizare venientem potest, quando et Baptisma unum sit, et Spiritus sanctus unus, et una Ecclesia a Christo Domino super Petrum origine unitatis et ratione fundata. Ita fit ut cum omnia apud illos inania et falsa sint, nihil eorum quae illi gesserint probari a nobis debeat. Quid enim potest ratum et firmum esse apud Deum quod illi faciunt quos Dominus hostes et adversarios suos dicit in Evangelio suo ponens: Qui non est mecum adversum me est; et qui non mecum colligit, spargit. Et beatus quoque apostolus Joannes mandata Domini et praecepta custodiens in epistola sua posuerit, dicens: Audistis quia antichristus venit. Nunc autem antichristi multi facti sunt. Unde cognoscimus quia novissima hora est. Ex nobis exierunt, sed non fuerunt ex nobis. Si enim fuissent ex nobis, mansissent utique nobiscum. Unde nos quoque colligere et considerare debemus an qui adversarii sunt Domini, et appellati sunt antichristi, possint dare gratiam Christi. Quare qui cum Domino sumus, et unitatem Domini tenemus et secundum ejus dignationem sacerdotium ejus in Ecclesia administramus, quaecumque adversarii ejus et antichristi faciunt repudiare et rejicere et pro profanis habere debemus, et eis qui de errore et pravitate venientes agnoscunt unius Ecclesiae veram fidem, dare illis per omnia divinae gratiae sacramenta et unitatis et fidei veritatem. Optamus vos, fratres charissimi, semper bene valere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p30","III.","IN THE YEAR OF CHRIST 256, IN THE PONTIFICATE OF STEPHEN III. CARTHAGINIAN COUNCIL UNDER CYPRIAN THE SIXTH, FROM THE PROVINCE OF AFRICA AND NUMIDIA, WHICH IS THE SECOND ON BAPTISM.","ANNO CHRISTI CCLVI, PONTIFICATUS S. STEPHANI III. CARTHAGINENSE CONCILIUM SUB CYPRIANO SEXTUM. EX AFRICA PROVINCIA ET NUMIDIA, QUOD DE BAPTISMO SECUNDUM EST."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p31","Prooemium.","He signifies that in the council he decreed that those who have been washed among heretics or schismatics, returning to the Church, should be baptized; and those who had been ordained presbyters or deacons in the Church and had lapsed into schism, or who had been promoted by heretics, should communicate as laymen when returning; the bond of concord, however, being preserved with bishops who thought otherwise.","Significat se in concilio decrevisse, ut qui apud haereticos aut schismaticos tincti sunt, redeuntes ad Ecclesiam baptizarentur; et qui in Ecclesia presbyteri vel diaconi ordinati, in schisma delapsi sint, aut qui ab haereticis promoti sint, revertentes ut laici communicarent: salvo tamen concordiae vinculo cum episcopis qui aliter sentirent."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p32","Prooemium.","To confirm more fully by a fuller sentence the things constituted in the preceding council, Cyprian, in the same cause of baptism conferred by heretics, in this same year 258, called together a fuller assembly of bishops, and convened for this synod 70 bishops from Africa and Numidia and one, to this end, that they might lay down what each thought concerning the traditions of the fathers to be kept holy and inviolably. After all the aforesaid had convened, they confirmed the same things which had been defined in the preceding African councils in this and the cause of Basilides, by a double decree issued thereupon. The first of which is, that those who were ordained in the Church and have fallen from the faith, returning to the Church, are to be received only to lay communion. The other, that the baptism of heretics conferred by them is to be held not only as invalid but also as null; so that if they return to the Church they are baptized, not rebaptized by a second baptism, but baptism is first conferred upon them, because they have not been truly baptized by heretics, but have been more defiled by the water of heretics. Thus Cyprian, Epistle 73, to Jubaianus. Whence those who think that he favored anabaptism gravely err. When these things had been constituted by the sentence of the aforesaid 71 bishops, they were prescribed to Stephen the Roman by a certain epistle, which we have taken care to subjoin here from Cyprian. He, however, having received the synodal epistle, reprobated this council by letters returned, and at the same time most holy commanded that they not depart a nail's breadth from the apostolic tradition of not repeating the baptism of heretics who had baptized in the same form which the Church uses, and that those baptized by them, if they seek to return to the Church, are to be received not otherwise than through the imposition of hands alone. These things are proven from Cyprian's epistle which he wrote to Pompeius of Sabrata in Africa, bishop. See Baronius, year 258, from number 27 to 38 inclusive.","Ut ea quae praecedente concilio constituta sunt pleniore sententia confirmentur, Cyprianus in eadem causa Baptismi ab haereticis collati, hoc eodem anno 258 pleniorem conventum episcoporum convocavit, et ex Africa et Numidia 70 episcopos et unum ad hanc synodum coegit, eo fine, ut quod singuli sentiunt de traditionibus majorum sancte et inviolabiliter servandis, deponant. Postquam omnes praedicti convenissent, idem, quod supra praecedentibus conciliis Africanis in hac et Basilidis causa habitis definitum est, duplici decreto desuper lato, confirmarunt. Quorum primum est, ut ii, qui in Ecclesia ordinati a fide defecerunt, revertentes ad Ecclesiam, non nisi ad laicam communionem recipiantur. Alterum, ut Baptismus haereticorum ab ipsis collatus, non tantum pro invalido, sed etiam pro nullo habeatur; adeo ut si ad Ecclesiam revertentes baptizentur, non secundo Baptismo rebaptizentur, sed tantum primum iis Baptisma conferatur, quia ab haereticis non vere baptizari, sed aqua tantum haereticorum amplius sordidati fuerint. Ita Cyprianus, Epist. 73, ad Jubaianum. Unde qui illum anabaptismo patrocinatum fuisse sentiunt, graviter errant. Haec cum episcoporum praedictorum 71 sententia constituta essent, ad Stephanum Romanum epistola quadam, quam infra ex Cypriano hic subjici curavimus, praescribuntur. Ille vero, synodica epistola accepta, per litteras redditas concilium hoc reprobavit, et simul etiam sanctissime mandavit, ne a traditione apostolica, de non iterando Baptismate haereticorum eorum, qui eadem forma, qua utitur Ecclesia, baptizassent, vel latum unguem recedant, ipsosque ab ipsis baptizatos, si ad Ecclesiam redire petant, non aliter quam per solam manuum impositionem recipiant. Haec ex Cypriani epistola, quam ad Pompeium Sabratensem in Africa episcopum scripsit, probantur. Vide Baron. anno 258, a num. 27 usque ad 38 inclusive."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p33","Cyprianus et Caeteri Stephano Fratri Salutem.","To arrange certain things and to be limited by the examination of common counsel, we have found it necessary, most dear brother, with many priests convening into one, to call together and celebrate a council. In which many things were indeed set forth and transacted, but concerning this especially we had to write to you, and to confer with your gravity and wisdom, which pertains more both to priestly authority and to the catholic unity and dignity equally of the Church coming from the ordinance of divine disposition, that those who are outside the Church, washed and stained with the stain of profane water among heretics and schismatics, when they come to us and to the Church, which is one, ought to be baptized; because it is too little for them to receive the Holy Spirit by imposition of hands, unless they also receive the baptism of the Church. For then at last they can be fully sanctified and be sons of God, if they be born again by both sacraments, since it is written: \"Unless a man be born again of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God\" (Jn 3:5). For we find also in the Acts of the Apostles that this was kept by the Apostles, and preserved by the truth of saving faith, that when upon the gentiles who were present there in the house of Cornelius the centurion, fervent with the heat of faith and believing in the Lord with their whole heart, the Holy Spirit had descended, by which they were filled and blessed God in various tongues, nevertheless the blessed Apostle Peter, mindful of the divine precept and the Gospel, commanded that those same who had already been filled with the Holy Spirit should be baptized, that nothing should seem to be omitted, that by all means the law of the divine precept and of the Gospel and the apostolic teaching might be preserved. But that baptism is not what heretics use, nor can anyone profit by the grace of Christ among those who are adversaries to Christ, has been recently diligently expressed in the epistle which was written to our colleague Quintus, constituted in Mauritania, concerning that matter; also in the letters which our colleagues before made to the bishops presiding in Numidia. Copies of both epistles I have subjoined.","Ad quaedam disponenda et consilii communis examinatione limanda necesse habuimus, frater charissime, convenientibus in unum pluribus sacerdotibus, cogere et celebrare concilium. In quo multa quidem prolata atque transacta sunt: sed de eo vel maxime tibi scribendum, et cum tua gravitate ac sapientia conferendum fuit, quod magis pertineat et ad sacerdotalem auctoritatem, et ad Ecclesiae catholicae unitatem pariter ac dignitatem de divinae dispositionis ordinatione venientem, eos qui sunt foris extra Ecclesiam tincti, et apud haereticos et schismaticos profanae aquae labe maculati, quando ad nos atque ad Ecclesiam, quae una est, venerint, baptizari oportere; eo quod parum sit eis manum imponere ad accipiendum Spiritum sanctum, nisi accipiant et Ecclesiae Baptismum. Tunc enim demum plene sanctificari et esse filii Dei possunt, si Sacramento utroque nascantur, cum scriptum sit, Nisi quis renatus fuerit ex aqua et Spiritu, non potest introire in regnum Dei. Invenimus enim etiam in Actis Apostolorum, hoc esse ab Apostolis custoditum, et salutaris fidei veritate servatum, ut cum in domo Cornelii centurionis super ethnicos qui illic aderant fidei calore ferventes, et in Dominum toto corde credentes descendisset Spiritus sanctus, quo adimpleti variis linguis Deum benedicerent; nihilominus tamen beatum apostolum Petrum, divini praecepti atque Evangelii memorem, praecepisse ut baptizarentur iidem illi, qui jam fuerant sancto Spiritu pleni: ut nihil praetermissum videretur, quominus per omnia divini praecepti atque Evangelii legem apostolica magisteria servarent. Baptismum autem non esse quo haeretici utuntur, nec quemquam apud eos, qui Christo adversantur, per gratiam Christi posse proficere, diligenter nuper expressum est in epistola, quae ad Quintum collegam nostrum in Mauritania constitutum super ea re scripta est, item in litteris quas collegae nostri ad episcopos in Numidia praesidentes ante fecerunt: cujus utriusque epistolae exempla subdidi."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p34","II.","We add plainly and join, most dear brother, by common consent and authority, that also if any presbyters or deacons, who either were first ordained in the catholic Church and afterwards stood perfidious and rebellious against the Church, or were promoted among heretics by pseudo-bishops and antichrists against the disposition of Christ by a profane ordination, and have tried to offer false and sacrilegious sacrifices outside against the one and divine altar, they also, on this condition, when they return, are to be received that they communicate as laymen, and let it suffice them that they are admitted to peace, who have been enemies of peace; nor ought they, when returning, to retain with us those arms of ordination and honor with which they rebelled against us. For priests and ministers who serve the altar and sacrifices ought to be whole and immaculate, since the Lord God speaks in Leviticus and says: \"A man in whom there is a blemish or defect shall not come to offer gifts to God\" (Lev 21:21); likewise in Exodus He commands the same and says: \"And the priests who approach the Lord God, let them be sanctified, lest the Lord God forsake them\" (Ex 19:22); and again: \"And when they approach to minister at the altar of the Holy, let them not bring iniquity upon themselves, lest they die\" (Ex 28:43). But what greater crime can be, or what uglier blemish, than to have stood against Christ, than to have dissipated His Church, which He prepared and founded with His blood, than, forgetful of evangelical peace and love, to have fought with the fury of hostile discord against the unanimous and concordant people of God? Even if these afterward return to the Church, they cannot restore and recall with themselves those who, seduced by them and overtaken by death outside, have perished outside the Church without communication and peace; whose souls will be demanded in the day of judgment from the hands of those who were the authors and leaders of perdition. And therefore it is enough for such returning to be given pardon; but perfidy ought not to be promoted in the house of faith. For what shall we reserve for the good and innocent and those not departing from the Church, if we honor those who have departed from us and stood against the Church?","Addimus plane et adjungimus, frater charissime, consensu et auctoritate communi, ut etiam si qui presbyteri aut diaconi, qui vel in Ecclesia catholica prius ordinati fuerint, et postmodum perfidi ac rebelles contra Ecclesiam steterint, vel apud haereticos a pseudoepiscopis et antichristis contra Christi dispositionem profana ordinatione promoti sint, et contra altare unum atque divinum sacrificia foris falsa ac sacrilega offerre conati sint, eos quoque hac conditione suscipi cum revertuntur, ut communicent laici, et satis habeant, quod admittuntur ad pacem, qui hostes pacis exstiterint; nec debere eos revertentes ea apud nos ordinationis et honoris arma retinere, quibus contra nos rebellaverint. Oportet enim sacerdotes et ministros, qui altari et sacrificiis deserviunt, integros atque immaculatos esse: cum Dominus Deus in Levitico loquatur et dicat: Homo in quo fuerit macula et vitium, non accedet offerre dona Deo; item in Exodo eadem praecipiat et dicat; Et sacerdotes qui accedunt ad Dominum Deum, sanctificentur, ne forte derelinquat illos Dominus Deus; et iterum: Et cum accedunt ministrare ad altare Sancti, non adducent in se delictum, ne moriantur. Quod autem majus potest esse delictum, aut quae macula deformior, quam adversus Christum stetisse, quam Ecclesiam ejus, quam ille sanguine suo paravit et condidit, dissipasse, quam evangelicae pacis ac dilectionis oblitum, contra unanimem et concordem Dei populum hostilis discordiae furore pugnasse? Qui etsi ipsi postmodum ad Ecclesiam redeunt, restituere tamen eos et secum revocare non possunt, qui ab eis seducti et foris morte praeventi extra Ecclesiam sine communicatione et pace perierunt: quorum animae in die judicii de ipsorum manibus expetentur, qui perditionis auctores et duces exstiterunt. Et idcirco satis est talibus revertentibus veniam dari: Non tamen debet in domo fidei perfidia promoveri. Nam quid bonis et innocentibus atque ab Ecclesia non recedentibus reservamus, si eos qui a nobis recesserint, et contra Ecclesiam steterint, honoramus?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p35","III.","These things we have brought to your conscience, most dear brother, both for the common honor and for simple love, believing that also to you, for your religion and the truth of faith, those things which are both religious and true are pleasing. But we know that some are unwilling to lay aside what they have once drunk in, nor easily change their purpose, but, the bond of peace and concord among colleagues being preserved, retain certain things which have once been usurped among themselves. In which matter we force no one nor give law, since each prelate has in the administration of the Church the free arbitrium of his own will, about to render an account of his act to the Lord. I wish you, most dear brother, always to be well.","Haec ad conscientiam tuam, frater charissime, et pro honore communi et pro simplici dilectione pertulimus, credentes etiam tibi pro religionis tuae et fidei veritate placere, quae et religiosa pariter et vera sunt. Caeterum scimus quosdam quod semel imbiberint nolle deponere, nec propositum suum facile mutare, sed salvo inter collegas pacis et concordiae vinculo, quaedam propria quae apud se semel sint usurpata retinere. Qua in re non vim cuiquam facimus aut legem damus, cum habeat in Ecclesiae administratione voluntatis suae arbitrium liberum unusquisque praepositus, rationem actus sui Domino redditurus. Opto te, frater charissime, semper bene valere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p36","III.","IN THE YEAR OF CHRIST 256, IN THE PONTIFICATE OF STEPHEN III. CARTHAGINIAN COUNCIL UNDER CYPRIAN THE SEVENTH, FROM THREE PROVINCES OF AFRICA, WHICH IS THE THIRD ON BAPTISM.","ANNO CHRISTI CCLVI, PONTIFICATUS S. STEPHANI III. CARTHAGINIENSE CONCILIUM SUB CYPRIANO SEPTIMUM, EX TRIBUS AFRICAE PROVINCIIS, QUOD DE BAPTISMO TERTIUM EST."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p37","Prooemium.","When, with persecution ceasing, they had convened at Carthage on the Kalends of September, very many bishops from the province of Africa, Numidia, and Mauritania, with presbyters and deacons, the greatest part of the people also being present, and the letters of Jubaianus to Cyprian had been read, likewise the replies of Cyprian to Jubaianus concerning heretics to be baptized, and what Jubaianus the same had afterward written back to Cyprian, Cyprian said: \"You have heard, most beloved colleagues, what Jubaianus, our fellow bishop, wrote to me, consulting our mediocrity concerning the illicit and profane baptism of heretics, and what I have written back to him, judging namely what we have once and again and often judged, that heretics coming to the Church ought to be baptized and sanctified by the baptism of the Church. Likewise the other letters of Jubaianus have been read to you, in which, with his sincere and religious devotion, writing back to our epistle, he not only consented but also confessed that he had been instructed and gave thanks. It remains that concerning this matter each of us set forth what we think, judging no one, nor removing anyone from the right of communication if he shall have thought differently. For no one of us makes himself a bishop of bishops, nor by tyrannical terror does he compel his colleagues to the necessity of obeying, since every bishop has, for the license of liberty and his own power, his own arbitrium, and he can no more be judged by another than he himself can judge another. But let us all await the judgment of our Lord Jesus Christ, who alone and singly has the power both of setting us in the governance of His Church and of judging concerning our act.\"","Cum in unum Carthagini convenissent Kalendis Septembris episcopi plurimi ex provincia Africa, Numidia, Mauritania, cum presbyteris et diaconibus, praesente etiam plebis maxima parte, et lectae essent litterae Jubaiani ad Cyprianum factae, item Cypriani ad Jubaianum rescriptae, de haereticis baptizandis, quidque postmodum Cypriano Jubaianus idem rescripserit, Cyprianus dixit: Audistis, collegae dilectissimi, quid mihi Jubaianus, coepiscopus noster, scripserit consulens mediocritatem nostram de illicito et profano haereticorum Baptismo, et quid ego ei rescripserim, censens scilicet, quod semel atque iterum et saepe censuimus, haereticos ad Ecclesiam venientes Ecclesiae Baptismo baptizari et sanctificari oportere. Item lectae sunt vobis et aliae Jubaiani litterae, quibus pro sua sincera et religiosa devotione ad epistolam nostram rescribens, non tantum consensit sed etiam instructum se esse confessus gratias egit. Superest ut de hac ipsa re singuli quid sentiamus proferamus, neminem judicantes, aut a jure communicationis aliquem, si diversum senserit, amoventes. Neque enim quisquam nostrum episcopum se episcoporum constituit, aut tyrannico terrore ad obsequendi necessitatem collegas suos adigit, quando habeat omnis episcopus pro licentia libertatis et potestatis suae arbitrium proprium, tamque judicari ab alio non possit quam nec ipse potest alterum judicare. Sed expectemus universi judicium Domini nostri Jesu Christi, qui unus et solus habet potestatem et praeponendi nos in Ecclesiae suae gubernatione et de actu nostro judicandi."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p38","Sententiae Episcoporum Lxxxvii, de Haereticis Baptizandis.","1. Caecilius a Bilta dixit: I know only one baptism in the Church, and none outside the Church. This one will be here. For the Church is like a dove, as the Lord says: \"My dove is one\" (Song 6:9). The Scripture says: \"A whore's hair is like a sponge,\" or like a crown, etc. And he says: \"The Son of man came not to destroy the souls of men, but to save\" (Lk 9:56). As far as we can, if it can be done, no soul is to be lost. And the Lord says in the Gospel: \"If you love me, keep my commandments\" (Jn 14:15). And blessed Paul says: \"But if any man preach to you a gospel other than that which you have received, let him be anathema\" (Gal 1:9). And the Lord says: \"He who is not with me is against me; and he who does not gather with me scatters\" (Lk 11:23). And blessed John the Apostle says: \"If anyone comes to you and does not bring this doctrine, do not receive him into your house, nor say to him, Hail\" (2 Jn 10). He who is against Christ, he is an adversary and antichrist. He who is with the adversaries of Christ, is he with Christ? He who gathers with those who scatter from the Church, does he gather with the Lord? He therefore judges that those baptized by adversaries and antichrists are to be baptized again by us. I judge that heretics ought to be baptized.","Caecilius a Bilta dixit: Ego unum Baptisma in Ecclesia solum scio, et extra Ecclesiam nullum. Hic erit. Ecclesia quasi columba est, dicente Domino: Una est columba mea. Scriptum est: Capillus meretricis sicut spongia, aut sicut corona, etc. Et dicit: Filius hominis non venit animas hominum perdere, sed salvare. Quantum possumus, si fieri potest, nulla anima perdenda est. Et Dominus in Evangelio dicit: Si diligitis me, mandata mea servate. Et beatus Paulus dicit: Sed si quis vobis evangelizaverit praeterquam quod accepistis, anathema sit. Et Dominus dicit: Qui non est mecum, adversus me est; et qui non mecum colligit, spargit. Et beatus Joannes apostolus dicit: Si quis venit ad vos et hanc doctrinam non affert, nolite eum recipere in domum, nec ei ave dixeritis. Qui contra Christum est, adversarius est et antichristus. Qui cum adversariis Christi est, cum Christo est? Qui cum spargentibus ab Ecclesia colligit, cum Domino colligit? Judicat ergo ab adversariis et antichristis baptizatos a nobis denuo baptizari oportere. Ego haereticos baptizari oportere censeo."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p39","Sententiae Episcoporum Lxxxvii, de Haereticis Baptizandis.","2. Primus a Misgirpa dixit: I judge that every man coming from heresy is to be baptized. For in vain does he think himself baptized there, since there is no baptism except in the one and true Church: because God is one, and faith is one, and the Church is one, in which stands one baptism and holiness and the rest. For those things which are performed outside have no effect of salvation.","Primus a Misgirpa dixit: Censeo omnem hominem ab haeresi venientem baptizandum. Frustra enim illic putat se esse baptizatum, cum non sit Baptisma nisi in Ecclesia unum et verum: quia et Deus unus, et fides una, et Ecclesia una est, in qua stat unum Baptisma et sanctitas, et caetera. Nam quae foris excercentur nullum habent salutis effectum."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p40","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","4. Novatus a Thamugade dixit: Although we know that all the Scriptures bear witness to saving Baptism, we ought nevertheless to express our faith, that heretics and schismatics coming to the Church, who are seen to have been pseudo-baptized, ought to be baptized in the perennial font; and therefore, according to the testimony of the Scriptures and according to the decree of our colleagues, men of most holy memory, all schismatics and heretics who are converted to the Church are to be baptized; but also those who were seen to have been ordained, are to be received among the laity.","Novatus a Thamugade dixit: Licet sciamus omnes Scripturas testimonium reddere de salutari Baptismo, debemus tamen fidem nostram exprimere, haereticos et schismaticos ad Ecclesiam venientes, qui pseudo-baptizati videntur, debere eos in fonte perenni baptizari, et ideo secundum testimonium Scripturarum et secundum decretum collegarum nostrorum sanctissimae memoriae virorum omnes schismaticos et haereticos qui ad Ecclesiam conversi sunt baptizari, sed et eos qui ordinati videbantur, inter laicos recipi."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p41","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","5. Nemesianus a Thubunis dixit: That the baptism which heretics and schismatics give is not true is everywhere declared in the holy Scriptures, because their prelates themselves are false christs and false prophets, the Lord saying through Solomon: \"He who trusts in false things feeds the winds; the same also follows flying birds. For he forsakes the ways of his own vineyard, and wanders from the paths of his own field. He enters, however, through dry and arid places, and a land destined for thirst; he gathers with his hands unfruitful things\" (Prov 9:12, 18, LXX). And again: \"Abstain from alien water, and do not drink from a foreign fountain, that you may live long, and years of life may be added to you.\" And in the Gospel the Lord our God Jesus Christ spoke with His divine voice, saying: \"Unless a man be born again of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God\" (Jn 3:5). This is the Spirit who from the beginning moved upon the waters. For neither can the Spirit operate without water, nor water without the Spirit. Therefore some wrongly interpret themselves, that they say that by the imposition of hands they receive the Holy Spirit and thus are received, whereas it is manifest that they ought to be born again in the catholic Church by both sacraments. Then indeed they can be sons of God, the Apostle saying: \"Careful to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace, one body and one Spirit, as you are called in one hope of your calling, one Lord, one faith, one baptism, one God\" (Eph 4:3-6). All these things the catholic Church speaks. And again in the Gospel the Lord says: \"That which is born of the flesh is flesh, and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit, because God is Spirit, and is born of God\" (Jn 3:6). Therefore all heretics and schismatics, whatever they do, are carnal, the Apostle saying: \"Now the works of the flesh are manifest, which are fornications, uncleanness, incest, idolatries, sorceries, enmities, contentions, zeal, anger, divisions, heresies, and the like; of which I forewarn you, as I have forewarned you, that they who do such things shall not obtain the kingdom of God\" (Gal 5:19-21). The Apostle therefore condemns with all evils both those who make division, that is, schismatics and heretics. Unless therefore they receive saving Baptism in the catholic Church, which is one, they cannot be saved, but with the carnal they shall be damned in the judgment of the Lord Christ.","Nemesianus a Thubunis dixit: Baptisma quod dant haeretici et schismatici non esse verum ubique in Scripturis sanctis declaratum est: quoniam ipsi praepositi eorum falsi christi sunt et falsi prophetae, dicente Domino per Salomonem: Qui fidens est in falsis, hic pascit ventos, idem autem ipse sequitur aves volantes. Deserit enim vias vineae suae, a semitis vero agelli sui erravit. Ingreditur autem per avia loca atque arida et terram destinatam in sitim, contrahit autem manibus infructuosa. Et iterum: Ab aqua aliena abstine te, et de fonte extraneo ne biberis, ut longum vivas tempus, adjiciantur etiam tibi anni vitae. Et in Evangelio divina sua voce Dominus noster Jesus Christus locutus est dicens: Nisi quis renatus fuerit ex aqua et Spiritu non potest introire in regnum Dei. Hic est Spiritus qui ab initio ferebatur super aquas. Neque enim Spiritus sine aqua operari potest, neque aqua sine Spiritu. Male ergo sibi quidam interpretantur ut dicant quod per manus impositionem Spiritum sanctum accipiant et sic recipiantur, cum manifestum sit utroque Sacramento debere eos renasci in Ecclesia catholica. Tunc quippe poterunt filii Dei esse, dicente Apostolo: Curantes servare unitatem Spiritus in conjunctione pacis, unum corpus, et unus Spiritus, sicut vocati estis in una spe vocationis vestrae, unus Dominus, una fides, unum Baptisma, unus Deus. Haec omnia Ecclesia catholica loquitur. Et iterum in Evangelio dicit Dominus: Quod natum est de carne caro est, et quod natum est de Spiritu spiritus est; quia Deus Spiritus est, et de Deo natus est. Ergo omnes haeretici et schismatici omnia quaecumque faciunt carnalia sunt, dicente Apostolo: Manifesta enim sunt opera carnis, quae sunt, fornicationes, immunditiae, incestum, idololatriae, veneficia, inimicitiae, certamina, zelus, ira, divisiones, haereses, et his similia; de quibus praedixi vobis, sicut praedico, quoniam quicumque faciunt regnum Dei non haereditabunt. Condemnat itaque Apostolus cum omnibus malis et eos qui divisionem faciunt, hoc est schismaticos et haereticos. Nisi ergo acceperint Baptisma salutare in Ecclesia catholica, quae una est, salvi esse non possunt, sed cum carnalibus in judicio Domini Christi damnabuntur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p42","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","6. Januarius a Lambese dixit: According to the authority of the holy Scriptures, I decree that all heretics are to be baptized, and thus to be admitted into the holy Church.","Januarius a Lambese dixit: Secundum Scripturarum sanctarum auctoritatem decerno haereticos omnes baptizandos, et sic in Ecclesiam sanctam admittendos."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p43","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","7. Lucius a Castra Galbae dixit: Since the Lord said in His Gospel: \"You are the salt of the earth; but if the salt loses its savor, wherewith shall it be salted? It is thenceforth good for nothing, but to be cast out and trodden under foot by men\" (Mt 5:13); and again, after His resurrection, sending His Apostles, He commanded, saying: \"All power is given to me in heaven and on earth. Go and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\" (Mt 28:18-19). Since therefore it is manifest that heretics, that is, enemies of Christ, do not have the whole confession of the Sacrament, likewise schismatics cannot be seasoned with spiritual wisdom, since they, departing from the Church which is one, having become foolish, are made contrary, let it be done as it is written: \"The houses of those who are contrary to the law ought to be cleansed,\" and it follows that those who have been defiled by those who are contrary ought first to be cleansed and then finally to be baptized.","Lucius a Castra Galbae dixit: Cum Dominus in Evangelio suo dixerit: Vos estis sal terrae, si autem sal infatuatum fuerit, in quo salietur? Ex eo ad nihilum valebit nisi ut projiciatur foras et conculcetur ab hominibus; et iterum post resurrectionem suam mittens Apostolos suos, mandaverit dicens: Data est mihi omnis potestas in coelo et in terra. Ite, et docete omnes gentes, baptizantes eos in nomine Patris et Filii et Spiritus sancti. Cum ergo manifestum sit haereticos, id est hostes Christi, non integram Sacramenti confessionem habere, item schismaticos non posse condire sapientia spiritali, cum ipsi ab Ecclesia, quae una est, recedendo infatuati contrarii facti sint, fiat sicut scriptum est, Domus contrariorum legis debet emundationem, et consequens est eos qui a contrariis baptizati inquinati sunt primo purgari et tunc demum baptizari."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p44","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","8. Crescens a Cirta dixit: In so great an assembly of most holy fellow priests, the letters of our most beloved Cyprian to Jubaianus having been read, likewise to Stephen, which contain so many testimonies of holy witnesses descending from the divine Scriptures that all, united by the grace of God, ought justly to consent, I judge that all heretics and schismatics who wish to come to the catholic Church are not to enter unless they have first been exorcised and baptized, excepting of course those who were baptized before in the catholic Church, yet in such a way that they be reconciled to the Church by the imposition of hands in penance.","Crescens a Cirta dixit: In tanto coetu sanctissimorum consacerdotum, lectis litteris Cypriani dilectissimi nostri ad Jubaianum, itemque ad Stephanum, quae tantum in se sanctorum testimoniorum descendentium ex Scripturis deificis continent ut merito omnes per Dei gratiam adunati consentire debeamus, censeo omnes haereticos et schismaticos qui ad catholicam Ecclesiam voluerint venire non ante ingredi nisi exorcizati et baptizati prius fuerint, exceptis his sane qui in Ecclesia catholica fuerint ante baptizati, ita tamen ut per manus impositionem in poenitentiam Ecclesiae reconcilientur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p45","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","9. Nicomedes a Segermis dixit: My sentence is this, that heretics coming to the Church are to be baptized, because outside, among sinners, they obtain no remission of sins.","Nicomedes a Segermis dixit: Mea sententia haec est, ut haeretici ad Ecclesiam venientes baptizentur, eo quod nullam foris apud peccatores remissionem peccatorum consequantur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p46","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","10. Munnulus a Girba dixit: The truth of our mother the catholic Church has always remained with us, brothers, and remains, and especially in the Trinity of Baptism, our Lord saying: \"Go and baptize the nations in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\" (Mt 28:19). Since therefore we manifestly know that heretics have neither Father nor Son nor Holy Spirit, they ought when coming to our mother Church truly to be born again and baptized, that the cancer which they had, and the wrath of damnation, and the effect of error, may be sanctified by the holy and heavenly laver.","Munnulus a Girba dixit: Ecclesiae catholicae matris nostrae veritas semper apud nos, fratres, et mansit et manet, et vel maxime in Baptismatis trinitate, Domino nostro dicente: Ite et baptizate Gentes in nomine Patris et Filii et Spiritus sancti. Cum ergo manifeste sciamus haereticos non habere nec Patrem nec Filium nec Spiritum sanctum, debent venientes ad Ecclesiam matrem nostram vere renasci et baptizari, ut cancer quod habebant et damnationis ira et erroris offectura per sanctum et coeleste Lavacrum sanctificetur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p47","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","11. Secundinus a Cedias dixit: Since our Lord Christ says: \"He who is not with me is against me\" (Mt 12:30), and John the Apostle calls those who depart from the Church antichrists (1 Jn 2:18-19), undoubtedly the enemies of Christ, and those who are named antichrists, cannot administer the grace of saving Baptism. And therefore I judge that those who flee from the snares of heretics to the Church are to be baptized by us, who have been called friends of God by His condescension (Jn 15:15).","Secundinus a Cedias dixit: Cum Dominus noster Christus dicat: Qui non est mecum adversus me est, et Joannes apostolus eos qui ab Ecclesia exeunt antichristos dicat, indubitanter hostes Christi, quique antichristi nominati sunt, gratiam Baptismi salutaris ministrare non possunt. Et ideo censeo eos qui de insidiis haereticorum confugiunt ad Ecclesiam baptizandos esse a nobis, qui amici appellati sumus Dei de ejus dignatione."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p48","3. Polycarpus ab Adrumeto dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics make ","12. Felix a Bagai dixit: As the blind leading the blind both fall into the ditch (Mt 15:14), so a heretic baptizing a heretic both fall into death. And therefore the heretic is to be baptized and brought to life, lest we, being alive, communicate with the dead.","Felix a Bagai dixit: Sicut caecus caecum ducens simul in foveam cadunt, ita haereticus haereticum baptizans simul in mortem cadunt. Et ideo haereticus baptizandus est, et vivificandus, ne nos vivi mortuis communicemus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p49","13. Polianus a Mileo dixit: It is just that a heretic be baptized in the holy Ch","14. Theogenes ab Hippone regio dixit: According to the Sacrament of heavenly grace which we have received, we believe one Baptism which is in the holy Church.","Theogenes ab Hippone regio dixit: secundum Sacramentum Dei gratiae coelestis, quod accepimus, unum Baptisma quod est in Ecclesia sancta credimus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p50","13. Polianus a Mileo dixit: It is just that a heretic be baptized in the holy Ch","15. Dativus a Badis dixit: We, as far as in us is, do not communicate with heretics, unless they have been baptized in the Church and have received remission of sins.","Dativus a Badis dixit: Nos quantum in nobis est haereticis non communicamus, nisi baptizati in Ecclesia fuerint, et remissionem peccatorum acceperint."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p51","13. Polianus a Mileo dixit: It is just that a heretic be baptized in the holy Ch","16. Successus ab Abbir Germaniciana dixit: Heretics either have no power or have all power. If they can baptize, they can also give the Holy Spirit. But if they cannot give the Holy Spirit, because they do not have the Holy Spirit, they cannot baptize spiritually either. Therefore we judge that heretics are to be baptized.","Successus ab Abbir Germaniciana dixit: Haereticis aut nihil licet aut totum licet. Si possunt baptizare, possunt dare et Spiritum sanctum. Si autem Spiritum sanctum dare non possunt, quia non habent Spiritum sanctum, nec baptizare spiritaliter possunt. Propterea censemus haereticos baptizandos esse."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p52","13. Polianus a Mileo dixit: It is just that a heretic be baptized in the holy Ch","17. Fortunatus a Tuchabori dixit: Jesus Christ the Lord and our God, the Son of God the Father and Creator (Mt 16:18), built His Church upon a rock, not upon heresy, and gave the power of baptizing to bishops, not to heretics. Therefore those who are outside the Church and standing against Christ scatter His sheep and flock, cannot baptize outside.","Fortunatus a Tuchabori dixit: Jesus Christus Dominus et Deus noster Dei Patris et creatoris Filius super petram aedificavit Ecclesiam suam, non super haeresim, et potestatem baptizandi episcopis dedit, non haereticis. Quare qui extra Ecclesiam sunt, et contra Christum stantes oves ejus et gregem spargunt, baptizare foris non possunt."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p53","13. Polianus a Mileo dixit: It is just that a heretic be baptized in the holy Ch","18. Sedatus a Tuburbo dixit: In as much as water sanctified by the priest's prayer in the Church washes away sins, so much does the heretic's speech, infected as if with a cancer, heap up sins. Therefore all peaceful forces must be used lest anyone, infected with heretical error and washed, refuse to receive the singular and true Baptism of the Church; without which whoever has not been baptized will be alien from the kingdom of heaven.","Sedatus a Tuburbo dixit. In quantum aqua sacerdotis prece in Ecclesia sanctificata abluit delicta, in tantum haeretico sermone velut cancere infecta cumulat peccata. Quare omnibus pacificis quidem viribus nitendum est ne quis haeretico errore infectus et tinctus singulare et verum Ecclesiae Baptismum detrectet accipere; quo quisquis non fuerit baptizatus regno coelorum fiet alienus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p54","13. Polianus a Mileo dixit: It is just that a heretic be baptized in the holy Ch","19. Privatianus a Sufetula dixit: He who says that heretics have the power of baptizing, let him first say who founded heresy. For if heresy is from God, it can also have divine indulgence. But if it is not from God, how can it have or confer the grace of God on anyone?","Privatianus a Sufetula dixit: Qui haereticos potestatem baptizandi habere dicit, dicat prius quis haeresim condiderit. Si enim haeresis a Deo est, habere et indulgentiam divinam potest. Si vero a Deo non est, quomodo gratiam Dei aut habere aut conferre alicui potest?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p55","20. Privatus a Sufibus dixit: He who approves the baptism of heretics, what else","21. Hortensianus a Laribus dixit: How many baptisms there are, let either the presumptors or the favorers of heretics look to. We vindicate for the Church one baptism, which we know only in the Church. Or how can they baptize anyone in the name of Christ whom Christ Himself calls His adversaries?","Hortensianus a Laribus dixit: Quot sint Baptismi viderint aut praesumptores aut fautores haereticorum. Nos unum Baptisma, quod non nisi in Ecclesia novimus, Ecclesiae vindicamus. Aut quomodo possunt in nomine Christi aliquem baptizare quos ipse Christus dicit adversarios suos esse?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p56","20. Privatus a Sufibus dixit: He who approves the baptism of heretics, what else","22. Cassius a Macomadibus dixit: Since there cannot be two baptisms, he who concedes baptism to heretics takes it away from himself. I therefore judge that the mournful and wasted heretics are to be baptized when they begin to come to the Church, and, washed by the sacred and divine washing and illuminated by the light of life, not enemies but peaceful, not aliens but domestics of the faith of the Lord, not adulterers but sons of God, not of error but of salvation, to be received into the Church, excepting those who, being faithful in the Church, had passed over to the darkness of heresy, but these are to be restored by the imposition of hands.","Cassius a Macomadibus dixit: Cum baptismata duo esse non possint, qui haereticis Baptisma concedit sibi tollit. Censeo itaque flebiles et tabidos haereticos baptizandos esse cum ad Ecclesiam venire coeperint, et sacra et divina lavatione lotos, et lumine vitae illuminatos, non hostes, sed pacificos, non alienos, sed fidei Domini domesticos, non adulteros, sed Dei filios, non erroris sed salutis effectos, in Ecclesiam recipi, absque his qui de Ecclesia fideles supplantati ad haeresis tenebras transierant, sed eos per manus impositionem restituendos."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p57","20. Privatus a Sufibus dixit: He who approves the baptism of heretics, what else","23. Alius Januarius a Vico Caesaris dixit: Since the Lord says in His Gospel: \"If you do not obey, you shall be devoured by the sword\" (Is 1:20); and the Apostle: \"But if I preach the Gospel, it is no glory to me, for necessity is laid upon me\" (1 Cor 9:16), we who are present to the Church, in which we preside, vindicating her baptism for her alone, ought to baptize those whom the Church has not baptized.","Alius Januarius a Vico Caesaris dixit: Si non obtemperat, in gladio devorabimini, dicit Dominus in Evangelio; et Apostolus: Si autem evangelizavero, non est mihi gloria, necessitas enim mihi incumbit. Et ideo nos Ecclesiae assistimus, in qua praesidemus, ut baptismum ejus ipsi soli vindicantes, eos quos Ecclesia non baptizavit baptizemus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p58","20. Privatus a Sufibus dixit: He who approves the baptism of heretics, what else","24. Alius Secundinus a Carpis dixit: Are heretics Christians or not? If they are Christians, why are they not in the Church of God? If they are not Christians, how do they make Christians? Or to what purpose will the Lord's word pertain, saying: \"He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters\" (Mt 12:30)? Whence it is established that upon foreign sons and the offspring of antichrist the Holy Spirit cannot descend by the imposition of hands alone, since it is manifest that heretics do not have baptism.","Alius Secundinus a Carpis dixit: Haeretici christiani sunt, an non? Si christiani sunt, cur in Ecclesia Dei non sunt? Si christiani non sunt, quomodo christianos faciunt? Aut quo pertinebit sermo Domini dicentis: Qui non est mecum, adversus me est, et qui non mecum colligit spargit? Unde constat super filios alienos et soboles antichristi Spiritum sanctum per manus impositionem tantummodo non posse descendere, cum manifestum sit haereticos Baptisma non habere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p59","25. Victoricus a Thabraca dixit: If it is lawful for heretics to baptize and giv","26. Alius Felix ab Uthina dixit: No one doubts, most holy fellow priests, that human presumption cannot do as much as the adorable and venerable majesty of our Lord Jesus Christ. Therefore, mindful of the peril, we ought not only to observe this, but also for it to be confirmed by all of us, that all heretics who run to the bosom of mother Church be baptized, that the heretical mind, which has been polluted by long-standing decay, may be purified by the sanctification of the Laver and reformed for the better.","Alius Felix ab Uthina dixit: Nemini dubium est, sanctissimi consacerdotes, non tantum posse humanam praesumptionem quantum Domini nostri Jesu Christi adorandam et venerabilem majestatem. Memores ergo periculi, hoc quoque non tantum observare debemus, verum etiam ab omnibus nobis confirmari oportet ut omnes haeretici qui ad sinum matris Ecclesiae accurrunt baptizentur, ut mens haeretica, quae diuturna tabe polluta est, sanctificatione Lavacri purgata in melius reformetur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p60","25. Victoricus a Thabraca dixit: If it is lawful for heretics to baptize and giv","27. Quietus a Baruch dixit: We who live by faith ought with believing observance to obey those things which have been foretold for our instruction. For it is written in Solomon (Ecclus 34:25): \"He who is baptized by the dead, what does his washing profit him?\" Which certainly speaks of those who are washed by heretics and of those who wash them. For if those who are baptized among them obtain eternal life by remission of sins, why do they come to the Church? But if nothing of salvation is received from a dead man, and therefore, recognizing their former error, they return with penance to the truth, they ought to be sanctified by the one life-giving Baptism which is in the catholic Church.","Quietus a Baruch dixit: Qui fide vivimus, obsequi iis quae instruendis nobis ante praedicta sunt credula observatione debemus. Nam scriptum est apud Salomonem: Qui baptizatur a mortuo, quid proficit lavatio ejus? Quod utique de his qui ab haereticis tinguntur et de tingentibus loquitur. Si enim qui apud illos baptizantur per remissionem peccatorum vitam aeternam consequuntur, cur ad Ecclesiam veniunt? Si vero a mortuo salutis nihil accipitur, ac proptera agnito errore pristino ad veritatem cum poenitentia revertuntur, uno vitali Baptismate, quod in Ecclesia catholica est, sanctificari debebunt."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p61","25. Victoricus a Thabraca dixit: If it is lawful for heretics to baptize and giv","28. Castus a Sicca dixit: He who, despising truth, presumes to follow custom, is either envious and malignant toward the brothers to whom truth is revealed, or ungrateful toward God, by whose inspiration His Church is instructed.","Castus a Sicca dixit: Qui, contempta veritate, praesumit consuetudinem sequi, aut circa fratres invidus est et malignus, quibus veritas revelatur, aut circa Deum ingratus est, cujus inspiratione Ecclesia ejus instruitur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p62","25. Victoricus a Thabraca dixit: If it is lawful for heretics to baptize and giv","29. Euchratius a Thenis dixit: Our faith and the grace of Baptism and the rule of ecclesiastical law, God and our Lord Jesus Christ, teaching His Apostles, completed with His own mouth, saying: \"Go and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\" (Mt 28:19). Therefore the false and iniquitous baptism of heretics is to be expelled by us and to be rejected with all detestation, from whose mouth venom, not life, nor heavenly grace, but blasphemy of the Trinity is expressed. And therefore it is manifest that heretics coming to the Church ought to be baptized with the whole and catholic Baptism, that cleansed from the blasphemy of their presumption they may be able to be reformed by the grace of the Holy Spirit.","Euchratius a Thenis dixit: Fidem nostram et Baptismatis gratiam et legis ecclesiasticae regulam Deus et Dominus noster Jesus Christus suo ore Apostolos docens perimplevit dicens: Ite, et docete omnes Gentes, baptizantes eos in nomine Patris et Filii et Spiritus sancti. Falsum ergo haereticorum Baptisma et iniquum a nobis pellendum est et omni detestatione refutandum, de quorum ore virus, non vita, nec gratia coelestis, sed blasphemia Trinitatis exprimitur. Et ideo venientes ad Ecclesiam haereticos integro et catholico Baptismate baptizari debere manifestum est, ut a blasphemia suae praesumptionis purificati possint Spiritus sancti gratia reformari."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p63","25. Victoricus a Thabraca dixit: If it is lawful for heretics to baptize and giv","30. Libosus a Vaga dixit: In the Gospel the Lord says: \"I am the truth\" (Jn 14:6). He did not say: \"I am custom.\" Therefore, truth having been made manifest, let custom yield to truth, so that even if in the past someone in the Church did not baptize heretics, now he should begin to baptize them.","Libosus a Vaga dixit: In Evangelio Dominus, Ego sum, inquit, veritas. Non dixit: Ego sum consuetudo. Itaque, veritate manifestata, cedat consuetudo veritati, ut etsi in praeteritum quis in Ecclesia haereticos non baptizabat nunc baptizare incipiat."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p64","25. Victoricus a Thabraca dixit: If it is lawful for heretics to baptize and giv","31. Lucius a Thebeste dixit: Heretics, blasphemous and iniquitous, tearing apart the sacred and adorable words of the Scriptures with various words, I judge to be execrable, and therefore to be exorcised and baptized.","Lucius a Thebeste dixit: Haereticos blasphemos atque iniquos verbis variis decerpentes sancta et adorabilia Scripturarum verba execrandos censeo, et ideo exorcizandos et baptizandos esse."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p65","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","33. Item alius Felix ab Amaccora dixit: I myself also, following the authority of the divine Scriptures, judge that heretics are to be baptized, but also those who contend that they have been baptized among schismatics. For if, according to the caution of Christ, our private fountain is one, let all the adversaries of our Church understand that he cannot be alien, nor can he who is the shepherd of one flock give saving water to two peoples. And therefore it is manifest that neither heretics nor schismatics can receive anything heavenly, who dare to receive from sinful men and from strangers to the Church. When there is no place for the giver, certainly it does not profit the receiver either.","Item alius Felix ab Amaccora dixit: Et ego ipse secutus divinarum Scripturarum auctoritatem baptizandos haereticos esse censeo, sed et eos qui apud schismaticos baptizatos se esse contendunt. Si enim secundum cautum Christi privatus fons noster est, intelligant cuncti Ecclesiae nostrae adversarii quia alienus esse non potest nec duobus populis salutarem aquam tribuere potest ille qui unius gregis pastor est. Et ideo manifestum est nec haereticos nec schismaticos aliquid coeleste posse suscipere, qui a peccatoribus hominibus et ab Ecclesia extraneis audeant accipere. Quando danti locus non est, utique nec accipienti prodest."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p66","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","34. Item alius Januarius Muzulensis dixit: I wonder that when all confess that there is one baptism, not all understand the unity of the same baptism. For the Church and heresy are two and different things. If heretics have baptism, we do not have it. But if we have it, heretics cannot have it. But it is not doubtful that the Church alone possesses the baptism of Christ, which alone possesses the grace of Christ and the truth.","Item alius Januarius Muzulensis dixit: Miror quod cum omnes confiteantur unum esse baptisma, non omnes intelligant ejusdem baptismatis unitatem. Ecclesia enim et haeresis duae et diversae res sunt. Si haeretici habent baptisma, nos non habemus. Si autem nos habemus, haeretici habere non possunt. Dubium autem non est Ecclesiam solam baptismum Christi possidere, quae sola possideat et gratiam Christi et veritatem."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p67","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","35. Adelphius a Thasvalte dixit: Without cause do some impugn the truth with a false and invidious word, to say that we rebaptize, when the Church does not rebaptize heretics but baptizes them.","Adelphius a Thasvalte dixit: Sine causa quidam falso et invidioso verbo impugnant veritatem ut rebaptizare nos dicant, quando Ecclesia haereticos non rebaptizet, sed baptizet."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p68","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","36. Demetrius a Leptiminus dixit: We keep one baptism, because we vindicate for the catholic Church alone her own thing. But those who say that heretics truly and legitimately baptize, they themselves are the ones who make not one but many baptisms. For since there are many heresies, according to their number baptisms will also be counted.","Demetrius a Leptiminus dixit: Unum baptisma nos custodimus, quia Ecclesiae catholicae soli rem suam vindicamus. Qui autem dicunt quia haeretici vere et legitime baptizant, ipsi sunt qui non unum sed multa baptismata faciunt. Nam cum haereses multae sint, pro earum numero et baptismata computabuntur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p69","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","37. Vincentius a Thibari dixit: We know heretics to be worse than gentiles. If therefore, converted to the Lord, they wish to come, we have indeed the rule of truth which the Lord commanded by divine precept to the Apostles, saying: \"Go, in my name lay hands, cast out devils\" (Mt 10:8). And in another place: \"Go, teach the nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\" (Mt 28:19). Therefore first by the imposition of hands in exorcism, second by the regeneration of baptism, then can they come to the promise of Christ. Otherwise I judge it ought not to be done.","Vincentius a Thibari dixit: Haereticos scimus esse pejores quam ethnicos. Si ergo conversi ad Dominum venire voluerint, habemus utique regulam veritatis, quam Dominus praecepto divino mandavit Apostolis dicens: Ite, in nomine meo manum imponite, daemonia expellite. Et alio loco: Ite, et docete gentes, baptizantes eos in nomine Patris et Filii et Spiritus sancti. Ergo primo per manus impositionem in exorcismo, secundo per baptismi regenerationem, tunc possunt ad Christi pollicitationem venire. Alias autem fieri censeo non debere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p70","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","38. Marcus a Mactari dixit: It is not to be wondered if heretics, enemies and impugners of truth, claim for themselves the power and dignity of another. But this is to be wondered, that some of our prevaricators of truth favor heretics and oppose Christians. Therefore we decree that heretics are to be baptized.","Marcus a Mactari dixit: Mirandum non est si haeretici hostes atque impugnatores veritatis rem sibi vindicant potestatis et dignationis alienae. Sed illud mirandum est quod quidam nostri praevaricatores veritatis haereticis suffragantur et christianis adversantur. Propterea decernimus haereticos baptizandos esse."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p71","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","39. Sattius a Sicilibba dixit: If their sins are forgiven to heretics in baptism, they come to the Church without cause. For since on the day of judgment sins are what are punished, there is nothing that heretics can fear from the judgment of Christ, if they have obtained remission of sins.","Sattius a Sicilibba dixit: Si haereticis in baptismo sua peccata donantur, sine causa ad Ecclesiam veniunt. Nam cum in die judicii peccata sint quae puniuntur, nihil est quod timere possint haeretici de judicio Christi, si sunt remissionem peccatorum consecuti."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p72","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","40. Victor a Gor dixit: Since sins are remitted only in the baptism of the Church, he who admits a heretic to communion without baptism does both things against reason: neither does he cleanse the heretics, and he pollutes Christians.","Victor a Gor dixit: Cum peccata non nisi in Ecclesiae baptismo remittantur, qui haereticum ad communicationem sine baptismo admittit, utrumque contra rationem facit, nec haereticos purgat, et christianos inquinat."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p73","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","41. Aurelius ab Utica dixit: Since the Apostle says (1 Tim 5:22): \"Do not communicate with other men's sins,\" what else does he communicate with other men's sins than he who communicates with heretics without the baptism of the Church? And therefore I judge that heretics are to be baptized so that they may receive remission of sins, and thus be communicated with.","Aurelius ab Utica dixit: Cum dicat Apostolus non communicandum peccatis alienis, quid aliud quam peccatis alienis communicat qui haereticis sine Ecclesiae baptismo communicat? Et ideo censeo baptizandos esse haereticos ut accipiant remissam peccatorum, et sic illis communicetur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p74","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","42. Jambus a Germaniciana dixit: Those who approve the baptism of heretics disapprove ours, so that those who are not, I will not say washed, but befouled outside the Church, they deny ought to be baptized in the Church.","Jambus a Germaniciana dixit: Qui haereticorum baptisma probant, nostrum improbant, ut extra Ecclesiam, non dicam lotos, sed sordidatos, negent in Ecclesia baptizari oportere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p75","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","43. Lucianus a Rucuma dixit: It is written (Gen 1:4): \"And God saw the light, that it was good, and divided between light and darkness.\" If light and darkness can agree, then something can be common between us and heretics. Therefore I judge that heretics are to be baptized.","Lucianus a Rucuma dixit: Scriptum est: Et vidit Deus lucem quia bona est, et divisit inter lucem et tenebras. Si potest luci et tenebris convenire, potest nobis et haereticis aliquid esse commune. Propterea censeo haereticos baptizandos esse."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p76","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","44. Pelagianus a Luperciana dixit: It is written (1 Kings 18:21): \"Either the Lord God is, or Baal is God.\" So also now, either the Church is the Church, or heresy is the Church. But if heresy is not the Church, how can the baptism of the Church exist among heretics?","Pelagianus a Luperciana dixit: Scriptum est: Aut enim Dominus Deus est, aut Baal Deus est. Ita et nunc, aut Ecclesia Ecclesia est, aut Haeresis Ecclesia est. Porro si Haeresis Ecclesia non est, quomodo esse apud haereticos baptisma Ecclesiae potest?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p77","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","45. Jader a Midila dixit: We know that there is not one baptism except in the catholic Church. And therefore we ought not to admit a heretic unless he has been baptized among us, lest he think himself to have been baptized outside the catholic Church.","Jader a Midila dixit: Scimus non esse nisi unum baptisma in Ecclesia catholica. Et ideo non debemus admittere haereticum nisi baptizatus apud nos fuerit, ne se putet extra Ecclesiam catholicam baptizatum esse."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p78","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","46. Item alius Felix a Marazana dixit: One faith, one baptism, but of the catholic Church, which alone has the power to baptize.","Item alius Felix a Marazana dixit: Una fides, unum baptisma, sed Ecclesiae catholicae, cui soli licet baptizare."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p79","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","47. Paulus ab Obba dixit: It does not move me if someone does not vindicate the faith and truth of the Church, since the Apostle says (Rom 3:3): \"What if some of them have fallen from faith? Shall their unbelief make the faith of God without effect? God forbid. For God is true, but every man a liar.\" But if God is true, how can the truth of baptism exist among heretics, among whom God is not?","Paulus ab Obba dixit: Me non movet si aliquis Ecclesiae fidem et veritatem non vindicat, quando Apostolus dicat: Quid enim si exciderunt a fide quidam illorum? Numquid infidelitas eorum fidem Dei evacuavit? Absit. Est enim Deus verax, omnis autem homo mendax. Si autem Deus verax est, quomodo esse apud haereticos baptismi veritas potest, apud quos Deus non est?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p80","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","48. Pomponius a Dionysiana dixit: It is manifest that heretics cannot baptize and give remission of sins, who have not the power either to loose or to bind anything on earth.","Pomponius a Dionysiana dixit: Manifestum est haereticos non posse baptizare et remissionem peccatorum dare, qui potestatem non habent ut aut solvere aut ligare aliquid in terris possint."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p81","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","49. Venantius a Timisa dixit: If a husband, setting out on a journey, should commend his wife to his friend to be kept, that friend would keep her commended to him with as much diligence as he could, lest her chastity and holiness be adulterated by anyone. Christ the Lord and our God, setting out to the Father, commended His spouse to us. Shall we keep her incorrupt and inviolate, or shall we betray her integrity and chastity to adulterers and corruptors? For he who makes the baptism of the Church common with heretics, betrays the spouse of Christ to adulterers.","Venantius a Timisa dixit: Si maritus peraegre proficiscens amico suo commendasset uxorem suam custodiendam, commendatam sibi ille quanta posset diligentia conservaret, ne ab aliquo castitas ejus et sanctitas adulteraretur. Christus Dominus et Deus noster ad Patrem proficiscens sponsam suam nobis commendavit. Utrumne eam incorruptam et inviolatam custodiemus, an integritatem ejus et castitatem moechis et corruptoribus prodemus? Qui enim baptisma Ecclesiae commune cum haereticis facit, sponsam Christi adulteris prodit."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p82","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","50. Ahymnus ab Ausvaga dixit: We have received one baptism, and we keep and exercise it. But he who says that heretics also may baptize, makes two baptisms.","Ahymnus ab Ausvaga dixit: Nos unum baptisma accepimus, et ipsum tenemus et exercemus. Qui autem dicit quia et haereticis licet baptizare, ipse duo baptismata facit."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p83","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","51. Saturninus a Victoriana dixit: If it is lawful for heretics to baptize, they are excused and defended in doing illicit things. Nor do I see why Christ called them His adversaries, or the Apostle antichrists.","Saturninus a Victoriana dixit: Si licet haereticis baptizare, excusati sunt et defensi illicita facientes. Nec video quare eos aut Christus adversarios suos aut Apostolus antichristos appellaverit."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p84","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","52. Saturninus a Thucca dixit: Gentiles, although they worship idols, nevertheless acknowledge and confess the supreme God, the Father Creator. Against Him Marcion blasphemes, and some are not ashamed to approve the baptism of Marcion. How do such priests keep or vindicate the priesthood of God, who do not baptize the enemies of God, and thus communicate with them?","Saturninus a Thucca dixit: Gentiles quamvis idola colant, tamen summum Deum patrem creatorem cognoscunt et confitentur. In hunc Marcion blasphemat, et quidam non erubescunt Marcionis baptismum probare. Quomodo tales sacerdotes sacerdotium Dei aut servant aut vindicant qui hostes Dei non baptizant, et sic illis communicant?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p85","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","53. Marcellus a Zama dixit: Since sins are remitted only by the baptism of the Church, he who does not baptize a heretic communicates with a sinner.","Marcellus a Zama dixit: Cum peccata nonnisi Ecclesiae baptismo remittantur, qui haereticum non baptizat, peccatori communicat."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p86","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","54. Irenaeus ab Ululis dixit: If the Church does not baptize a heretic for this reason, that it says he has already been baptized, heresy is greater. But since we do not know what the judgment of the Lord may be, we ought to baptize, that he may have the remission of his sins.","Irenaeus ab Ululis dixit: Si ideo Ecclesia haereticum non baptizat quod dicatur jam baptizatus esse, Haeresis major est. Cum vero nesciamus quod judicium Domini futurum sit, baptizare debemus, ut remissionem peccatorum consequatur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p87","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","55. Donatus a Cibaliana dixit: I know one Church and one baptism of it. If there is anyone who says that the grace of baptism exists among heretics, let him first show and prove that there is the Church there.","Donatus a Cibaliana dixit: Ego unam Ecclesiam et unum baptisma ejus novi. Si est qui dicat esse apud haereticos baptismi gratiam, ante est ut ostendat et probet esse illic Ecclesiam."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p88","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","56. Zosimus a Tharassa dixit: Revelation of truth having been made, let error yield to truth; because even Peter, who first circumcised, yielded to Paul preaching the truth (Gal 3).","Zosimus a Tharassa dixit: Revelatione facta veritatis, cedat error veritati; quia et Petrus qui prius circumcidebat, cessit Paulo veritatem praedicanti."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p89","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","57. Julianus a Telepte dixit: It is written (Jn 3:29): \"No man can receive anything, unless it be given him from heaven.\" If heresy is from heaven, it can also give baptism.","Julianus a Telepte dixit: Scriptum est: Nemo potest aliquid accipere, nisi datum fuerit illi de coelo. Si Haeresis de coelo est, potest et baptisma dare."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p90","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","58. Faustus a Timida regia dixit: Let not those who favor heretics flatter themselves. He who interposes for heretics against ecclesiastical baptism makes them Christians and us heretics.","Faustus a Timida regia dixit: Non sibi blandiantur qui haereticis patrocinantur. Qui pro haereticis ecclesiastico baptismati intercedit, illos christianos et nos haereticos facit."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p91","32. Eugenius ab Ammedera dixit: I also judge the same, that heretics are to be b","59. Geminius a Furnis dixit: Some of my colleagues can prefer heretics to themselves, but they cannot to us. And therefore what we have once decreed we hold, that we baptize those coming from heretics.","Geminius a Furnis dixit: Quidam de collegis haereticos praeponere sibi possunt, nobis non possunt. Et ideo quod semel decrevimus tenemus, ut ab haereticis venientes baptizemus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p92","62. Item alius Lucius a Membresa dixit: It is written: \"God does not hear the si","63. Item alius Felix a Bussacenis dixit: In admitting heretics without the baptism of the Church, let no one prefer custom to reason and truth, because reason and truth always exclude custom.","Item alius Felix a Bussacenis dixit: In haereticis sine Ecclesiae baptismo admittendis nemo consuetudinem rationi et veritati praeponat, quia consuetudinem ratio et veritas semper excludit."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p93","62. Item alius Lucius a Membresa dixit: It is written: \"God does not hear the si","64. Alius Saturninus ab Avitinis dixit: If antichrist can give the grace of Christ to anyone, then heretics, who have been called antichrists, can also baptize.","Alius Saturninus ab Avitinis dixit: Si potest antichristus dare alicui gratiam Christi, possunt et haeretici baptizare, qui appellati sunt antichristi."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p94","65. Quintus ab Aggya dixit: He can give something who has something. But what ca","66. Alius Julianus a Marcelliana dixit: If a man can serve two masters, God and mammon (Mt 6:24), then baptism can serve two, a Christian and a heretic.","Alius Julianus a Marcelliana dixit: Si potest homo duobus dominis servire, Deo et mammonae, potest et baptisma duobus servire, christiano et haeretico."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p95","65. Quintus ab Aggya dixit: He can give something who has something. But what ca","67. Tenax ab Horreis Caeliae dixit: Baptism is one, but of the Church. Where the Church is not, baptism cannot be there.","Tenax ab Horreis Caeliae dixit: Baptisma unum est, sed Ecclesiae. Ubi Ecclesia non est, baptisma illic esse non potest."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p96","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","69. Donatulus a Capse dixit: I have always felt this also, that heretics, who can obtain nothing outside, when they are converted to the Church are to be baptized.","Donatulus a Capse dixit: Et ego semper hoc sensi, ut haeretici, qui nihil foris consequi possunt, quando ad Ecclesiam convertuntur baptizentur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p97","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","70. Verulus a Rusiccade dixit: A heretical man cannot give what he does not have; much more a schismatic, who has lost what he had.","Verulus a Rusiccade dixit: Homo haereticus dare non potest quod non habet; multo magis schismaticus, qui quod habuit amisit."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p98","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","71. Pudentianus a Cuiculi dixit: The newness of my episcopate has effected, most beloved brothers, that I should await what my elders might judge. For it is manifest that heresies have nothing nor can do anything. And so it is most justly decreed that if any come from them, they are to be baptized.","Pudentianus a Cuiculi dixit: Novitas episcopatus effecit, fratres dilectissimi, ut sustinerem quid majores judicarent. Nam haereses nihil habere nec posse manifestum est. Atque ita si qui ex eis venerint baptizari aequissime statutum est."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p99","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","72. Petrus ab Hippone Diarrhytorum dixit: Since baptism is one in the catholic Church, it is manifest that one cannot be baptized outside the Church. And therefore I judge that those washed in heresy or schism, coming to the Church, ought to be baptized.","Petrus ab Hippone Diarrhytorum dixit: Cum baptisma unum sit in catholica Ecclesia, manifestum est non posse extra Ecclesiam baptizari. Et ideo in Haeresi tinctos sive in schismate venientes ad Ecclesiam censeo baptizari oportere."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p100","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","73. Item alius Lucius ab Ausafa dixit: According to the motion of my mind and the Holy Spirit, since there is one God the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, and one Christ, and one hope, and one spirit, and one Church, there ought to be also one baptism. And therefore I say that if anything has been done or made by heretics, it is to be rescinded, and that those who come from them are to be baptized in the Church.","Item alius Lucius ab Ausafa dixit: Secundum motum animi mei et Spiritus sancti, cum sit unus Deus Pater Domini nostri Jesu Christi, et unus Christus, et una spes, et unus spiritus, et una Ecclesia, unum debet esse et baptisma. Et ideo dico, si quid ab haereticis motum aut factum fuerit, rescindi debere, et eos qui inde veniunt in Ecclesia baptizandos esse."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p101","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","74. Item alius Felix a Gurgitibus dixit: I judge that, according to the precepts of the holy Scriptures, one who has been illegitimately baptized by heretics outside the Church, when he wishes to flee to the Church, should obtain the grace of baptism where it is lawfully given.","Item alius Felix a Gurgitibus dixit: Ego censeo ut secundum sanctarum Scripturarum praecepta baptizatus ab haereticis illicite extra Ecclesiam, cum ad Ecclesiam confugere voluerit, gratiam baptismi ubi licite datur consequatur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p102","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","75. Pusillus a Lamasba dixit: I believe that saving baptism is not except in the catholic Church. Whatever is outside the catholic is a pretence.","Pusillus a Lamasba dixit: Ego credo baptisma salutare non esse nisi Ecclesia catholica. Quidquid absque catholica fuerit simulatio est."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p103","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","76. Salvianus a Gazaufala dixit: It is established that heretics have nothing. And therefore they come to us that they may be able to receive what they do not have.","Salvianus a Gazaufala dixit: Haereticos nihil habere constat: et ideo ad nos veniunt ut possint accipere quod non habent."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p104","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","77. Honoratus a Thucca dixit: Since Christ is truth, we ought to follow truth rather than custom, that heretics, who for this reason come to us because they could receive nothing outside, we may sanctify by the baptism of the Church.","Honoratus a Thucca dixit: Cum Christus veritas sit, magis veritatem quam consuetudinem sequi debemus, ut haereticos, qui ideo ad nos veniunt quia foris nihil accipere potuerunt, Ecclesiae baptismo sanctificemus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p105","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","78. Victor ab Octavo dixit: As you yourselves also know, I was not long ago made bishop, and therefore I awaited the counsel of my predecessors. This therefore I think, that whoever come from heresy should without doubt be baptized.","Victor ab Octavo dixit: Quod et ipsi scitis, non olim sum episcopus constitutus, et ideo expectabam praecessorum consilium. Hoc itaque existimo, ut quicumque ex Haeresi venerint sine dubio baptizentur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p106","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","79. Clarus a Mascula dixit: The sentence of our Lord Jesus Christ is manifest, sending His Apostles and permitting to them alone the power given to Him by the Father, to whom we succeed, governing the Church of the Lord with the same power and baptizing the faith of believers. And therefore heretics, who have neither power outside nor the Church of Christ, cannot baptize anyone with His baptism.","Clarus a Mascula dixit: Manifesta est sententia Domini nostri Jesu Christi Apostolos suos mittentis et ipsis solis potestatem a Patre sibi datam permittentis, quibus nos successimus eadem potestate Ecclesiam Domini gubernantes et credentium fidem baptizantes. Et ideo haeretici, qui nec potestatem foris nec Ecclesiam Christi habent, neminem baptizare baptismo ejus possunt."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p107","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","80. Secundianus a Thambeis dixit: We ought not by our presumption to deceive heretics, that, not baptized in the Church of our Lord Jesus Christ, and therefore not having obtained remission of sins, when the day of judgment shall come, it may be imputed to us that they were not baptized by us and did not obtain the indulgence of divine grace. Wherefore, since there is one Church and one baptism, when they are converted to us, let them together with the Church also obtain the baptism of the Church.","Secundianus a Thambeis dixit: Non debemus haereticos nostra praesumptione decipere ut in Ecclesia Domini nostri Jesu Christi non baptizati, ac per hoc remissionem peccatorum non consecuti, cum judicii dies venerit nobis imputetur quod per nos non sint baptizati et indulgentiam divinae gratiae non sint consecuti. Propter quod, cum sit una Ecclesia et unum baptisma, quando ad nos convertuntur, simul cum Ecclesia et Ecclesiae baptismum consequantur."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p108","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","81. Item alius Aurelianus a Chullabi dixit: John the Apostle put in his epistle, saying: \"If anyone comes to you and does not have the doctrine of Christ, do not receive him into your house, nor say to him, Hail. For he who says to him, Hail, communicates with his evil deeds\" (2 Jn 1:10-11). How can such be rashly admitted into the house of God who are forbidden to be admitted into our private house? Or how can we communicate with them without the baptism of the Church when, if we say only Hail to them, we communicate with their evil deeds?","Item alius Aurelianus a Chullabi dixit: Joannes apostolus in epistola sua posuit dicens: Si quis ad nos venit, et doctrinam Christi non habet, nolite eum admittere in domum vestram, et ave illi ne dixeritis. Qui enim dixerit illi ave communicat factis ejus malis. Quomodo admitti tales temere in Domum Dei possunt qui in domum nostram privatam admitti prohibentur? Aut quomodo cum eis sine Ecclesiae baptismo communicare possumus quibus si ave tantum dixerimus, factis eorum malis communicamus?"],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p109","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","82. Litteus a Gemellis dixit: The blind leading the blind, both fall into the ditch (Mt 15:14). Since therefore it is established that heretics, being blind, cannot illuminate anyone, their baptism is not valid.","Litteus a Gemellis dixit: Caecus si caecum ducat, ambo in foveam cadunt. Cum ergo haereticos constet non posse illuminare quemquam, utpote caecos, baptisma eorum non valet."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p110","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","83. Natalis ab Oea dixit: I, present as also Pompeius of Sabrata and Dioga of Leptimagna, who, although absent in body, present in spirit, committed to me their mandate, judge what our colleagues also judge, that heretics cannot have communication with us unless they have been baptized with ecclesiastical baptism.","Natalis ab Oea dixit: Tam ego praesens quam et Pompeius Sabratensis, quam etiam Dioga Leptimagnensis, qui mihi mandaverunt, corpore quidem absentes, spiritu praesentes, censemus quod et collegae nostri, quod haeretici communicationem habere nobiscum non possunt, nisi ecclesiastico Baptismo baptizati fuerint."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p111","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","84. Julius a Neapoli dixit: I do not depart from what we have once judged, that we baptize heretics coming to the Church.","Julius a Neapoli dixit: Ab eo quod semel censuimus non recedo, ut haereticos venientes ad Ecclesiam baptizemus."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p112","68. Alius Victor ab Assuras dixit: It is written that God is one, and Christ is ","85. Cyprianus a Carthagine dixit: My sentence is most fully expressed in the epistle which was written to our colleague Jubaianus, that heretics, according to the evangelical and apostolic testimony, having been called adversaries of Christ and antichrists, when they come to the Church, are to be baptized with the one baptism of the Church, that they may be able to be made from adversaries friends and from antichrists Christians.","Cyprianus a Carthagine dixit: Meam sententiam plenissime exprimit epistola quae ad Jubaianum collegam nostrum scripta est, haereticos secundum Evangelicam et apostolicam contestationem adversarios Christi et antichristos appellatos, quando ad Ecclesiam venerint, unico Ecclesiae baptismo baptizandos esse, ut possint fieri de adversariis amici et de antichristis Christiani."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p113","Conclusion","This council exists at page 329 of the Benedictine edition of St. Cyprian.","Exstat hoc concilium ad p. 329, edit. Benedictinae S. Cypriani."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p114","Conclusion","BOLLANDIST ANIMADVERSIONS ON THESE WORDS OF THE CARTHAGINIAN COUNCIL: \"For no one of us makes himself a bishop of bishops,\" etc.","BOLLANDISTARUM ANIMADVERSIONES IN HAEC CONCILII CARTHAGINENSIS VERBA: Neque enim quisquam nostrum episcopum se esse episcoporum constituit, etc."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p115","Conclusion","Which St. Cyprian relates concerning the \"Bishop of Bishops\" and the use of tyrannical error to the necessity of obeying, these same can be taken without any vice of moved spirit and injury of Pontifical Authority. St. Cyprian, namely, as a Primatial Pontiff, wished to admonish the gathered bishops that each should speak his own sentence freely. To this end, he observed that he himself was a colleague among all; and that although he held the primacy of Africa, neither he nor any other of those present was a bishop of bishops, or that such power be tyrannically arrogated to himself that he should force others to his own sentiments by his terror. But those things which he adds concerning the arbitrium and power of each bishop, namely that no one can be judged by another, nor can he judge another, these are to be referred to those same gathered bishops, and in a sound sense, or they manifestly contradict St. Cyprian's own deeds. Certainly St. Augustine, number 695, quoted by us, received those words thus: \"I think, of course, in those questions which have not yet been discussed with most refined perfection.\" But it was most true that that question concerning the baptism of heretics, which no general council up to that time had defined, and which rested on custom alone, that question, I say, could not be so decided by any of the bishops gathered with St. Cyprian that the others should be forced by his authority to depart from their own opinion. On the contrary, if anyone should contend that the Saint's words are to be taken generally, he must say by what right or by what injury the same Saint pronounced that Fortunatus of Assuritana in the proconsular province, bishop, in Epistle 64, and Basilides and Martialis, bishops of Spain, in Epistle 68, on account of the crime of idolatry, were rightly removed from office and to be expelled from their sees? By the same right he gave Epistle 67 to Stephen, the Roman Pontiff, that he should order Marcian, bishop of Arles in Gaul, to be cast out from his see and another to be substituted in it, if every bishop has for the license of liberty and his own power his own arbitrium, and he can no more be judged by another than he can judge another, and if therefore we must await the judgment of our Lord Jesus Christ, who alone and singly has the power both of setting them in the governance of His Church and of judging concerning their act? It cannot therefore be denied that the objecting words of St. Cyprian are to be restricted in a sound sense, or that he suffered something human from the heat of contention.","Quae S. Cyprianum de Episcopo Episcoporum ac tyrannici erroris ad obsequendi necessitatem usu memorat, . . . haec eadem absque ullo commoti animi vitio ac Pontificiae Auctoritatis injuria dicta accipi possunt. Volebat scilicet S. Pontifex Primas, congregatos episcopos de sua quemque sententia libere dicenda admonere. Hunc in finem, quod ad libertatem illam maxime conducere rebatur, observavit sese omnes collegas esse; et licet ipse primatum Africae teneret, nec se tamen, nec quemquam aliorum qui aderant, episcopum episcoporum esse, aut eam potestatem sibi tyrannice arrogari, ut caeteros terrore suo ad sua sensa adigat . . . . . Quae vero ibidem addit de cujusque episcopi arbitrio, ac potestate, videlicet nullum ab alio judicari posse, nec alium pariter judicare, haec ad eosdem illos congregatos, et quidem sano sensu referenda sunt, aut ipsis S. Cypriani gestis manifeste repugnant. Certe S. Augustinus, num. 695, a nobis relatus, ea verba sic excepit: Opinor utique in his quaestionibus, quae nondum eliquatissima perfectione discussae sunt. Verissimum autem erat, quaestionem illam de haereticorum Baptismate, quam nullum ad id usque tempus Concilium generale . . . definiverat, quaeque sola consuetudine nitebatur, verissimum, inquam, est, hanc quaestionem ab ullo ex episcopis cum S. Cypriano congregatis sic decidi non potuisse, ut ob illius auctoritatem caeteri ab opinione sua cogerentur recedere. Contra si quis Sancti dicta generaliter accipienda esse contendat, is igitur dicat, necesse est, quo jure, quave injuria idem Sanctus Fortunatum Assuritanum in provincia proconsulari Episcopum in Epist. 64, et Basilidem ac Martialem, Hispaniarum Episcopos in Epist. 68, ob scelus idololatriae recte exauctoratos, pronuntiaverit, et a sedibus suis arcendos curaverit? Quo idem jure Epistolam 67 ad Stephanum Romanum Pontificem dedit, ut Marcianum Arelatensem in Galliis Episcopum, sede sua dejici, aliumque in eam suffici juberet, si habeat omnis Episcopus pro licentia libertatis et potestatis suae arbitrium proprium, tamque judicari ab alio non possit, quam nec ipse potest alterum judicare, et si propterea exspectandum sit judicium Domini nostri Jesu Christi, qui unus et solus habet potestatem et praeponendi eos in Ecclesiae suae gubernatione, et de actu eorum judicandi? Negari igitur non potest, objecta S. Cypriani verba sano sensu restringenda esse, aut dicendum, eum ex contentionis ardore aliquid humani hic passum esse."],["vol3/carthage-cyprian/index.html","p116","Conclusion","Against the same primacy of the Roman Pontiff, the cause of Fortunatus proves nothing, concerning which St. Cyprian complains in Epistle 55, that he had sailed to Rome to St. Cornelius through Felicissimus and other associates of schism, to extort communion from him by falsehoods and calumnies; and was asked, if he wished to reply anything concerning the sentence passed on them, to present himself to his tribunal. For it is to be noted that Fortunatus was one of the five schismatic presbyters of Carthage, who, on account of the schism raised with Felicissimus, had already fled from the church and been condemned by African bishops, and then had been ordained a pseudo-bishop in Africa by heretics and schismatics; to such a degree that he could not be held as a bishop. St. Augustine testifies in Epistle 43, another 162, that in his own time also it was not permitted to presbyters and other clerics of inferior order to appeal to the apostolic See; which, however, he affirms was permitted to bishops, both there and elsewhere. Therefore it was agreed among African prelates that clerics inferior to bishops should be judged each in their own province, and this without doubt with the assent of the Roman Pontiff, lest appeals to the Apostolic See be too frequent and give occasion to unjust accusations. But just as this prohibition in the time of St. Augustine did not prevent him from acknowledging the power and primacy of the Roman Church, in which, as he says in the same place, the primacy of the apostolic chair always flourished; so neither can it be concluded from a similar deed of St. Cyprian against the same primacy. Moreover, it is false what Fellus, a heterodox bishop in England (and after him Cl. Boysen), impudently affixes to the most holy prelate: namely that, in the judgment of that blessed martyr, those are to be held as desperate and lost to whom the authority of the bishops in Africa seems less than that of Cornelius himself, sitting in the chair of Peter at Rome. For if anyone attentively considers the whole place, he will confess that the authority of the bishops in Africa is opposed by St. Cyprian not to St. Cornelius, but to a few desperate and lost men, namely the followers of Fortunatus and Felicissimus. For he subjoins: \"Their cause has already been judged, sentence has already been pronounced on them, nor does it fit the censure of priests to be reproved by the levity of a mobile and inconstant mind... If the number of those who judged them in the previous year, with the presbyters and deacons computed, they were more present at the judgment and examination than are these same who now seem to be joined with Fortunatus. For you ought to know, most dear brother, that after he was made a pseudo-bishop by heretics, he was already deserted by almost all.\" Thus St. Cyprian argued there as follows: it was decreed that clerics inferior to the episcopal order should plead their cause in their own diocese; but the cause of Fortunatus, Felicissimus, and the others has already been discussed among us, and sentence has already been pronounced on it, and therefore they are not to be heard at Rome, unless perhaps one should think that a few desperate and lost men are more to be believed than so great a number of bishops in Africa and other clerics who judged them in the previous year, and were many more than those who now, adhering to Fortunatus, have appealed to the apostolic see. This I think is the genuine sense of the words. But if anyone should contend that the words \"desperate and lost\" are to be taken not in the sense of removing, but of giving, yet even thus he will not extort Fellus's fiction, nor prove that the authority of the African prelates is here equalized to the pontifical, since the sense could then be: unless perhaps those few schismatics think that the authority of the African prelates is less than that they should subject themselves to their sentence according to what has been decreed concerning appeals. This is certain, that in the same place no comparison of the African sees with the Roman chair is instituted, and that Fortunatus and his followers are called desperate and lost because of heresy and their crimes, not because they had appealed to the Pontiff.","Adversus eumdem Romani Pontificis primatum nihil pariter probat causa Fortunati, de quo S. Cyprianus in epistola 55 queritur, quod per Felicissimum, aliosque schismatis socios, Romam ad S. Cornelium navigasset, ut mendaciis ac calumniis suis communionem ab eo extorqueret; postulatusque, ut, si quid ad latam in eos sententiam reponere velit, ad suum tribunal se sistat. Advertendum enim est, Fortunatum illum e quinque Presbyteris Carthaginensibus schismaticis unum fuisse, quem ob excitatum cum Felicissimo schisma jam ante de ecclesia profugum et ab Africanis episcopis damnatum, ac deinde ab haereticis schismaticisque pseudo-episcopum in Africa ordinatum fuisse, supra docuimus; adeoque hic pro episcopo haberi non poterat. Testatur autem S. Augustinus in epistola 43, alias 162, suo etiam tempore Presbyteris, caeterisque inferioris ordinis clericis permissum non fuisse ad apostolicam Sedem appellare; quod tamen episcopis licuisse, tum ibi, tum alibi affirmat. Convenerat itaque inter Africanos antistites, ut clerici episcopis inferiores in sua quique provincia judicarentur, idque hand dubie assentiente romano Pontifice, ne appellationes ad Sedem Apostolicam nimium frequentes essent, et injustis criminationibus occasionem darent. Ut vero haec prohibitio tempore S. Augustini non obstabat, quo minus hic tum alibi saepius, tum loco citato, romanae Ecclesiae, in qua semper, ut ibidem loquitur, apostolicae cathedrae viguit primatus, potestatem in caeteros Episcopos primatumque agnosceret; ita nec ex simili S. Cypriani facto contra eumdem primatum potest concludi. Falsum autem est, quod Fellus heterodoxus in Anglia episcopus (et post eum Cl. Boysen) sanctissimo praesuli impudenter affingit: scilicet, beato martyre illo judice, pro desperatis et perditis habendos esse, quibus minor videtur auctoritas episcoporum in Africa, quam vel Cornelii ipsius, in Petri cathedra Romae sedentis. Etenim, si quis totum locum, attente consideret, fatebitur, Episcoporum in Africa auctoritatem non S. Cornelio, sed paucis desperatis et perditis, Fortunati scilicet ac Felicissimi asseclis, a S. Cypriano opponi. Subdit enim: Jam causa eorum cognita est, jam de eis dicta sententia est, nec censurae congruit sacerdotum mobilis atque inconstantis animi levitate reprehendi . . . Si eorum, qui de illis priore anno judicaverunt, numerus cum presbyteris et diaconibus computetur, plures tunc affuerunt judicio, et cognitioni, quam sunt iidem isti, qui cum Fortunato nunc videntur esse conjuncti. Scire enim debes, frater charissime, eum posteaquam pseudoepiscopus ab haereticis factus est, jam pene ab omnibus esse desertum. Itaque S. Cyprianus ibidem sic fere argumentatus est: statutum est ut episcopali ordine inferiores clerici in dioecesi sua causam agerent: atqui Fortunati, Felicissimique ac caeterorum causa apud nos discussa, de eaque jam dicta sententia est, atque adeo audiendi illi Romae non sunt, nisi forte quis paucis illis desperatis et perditis magis credendum existimaverit, quam tanto episcoporum in Africa, aliorumque clericorum numero, qui priori anno de iis judicarunt, multoque plures fuerunt. quam qui nunc Fortunato adhaerentes, ad sedem apostolicam appellarunt. Hanc ego genuinam verborum sententiam esse existimo: at si quis voces istas paucis desperatis et perditis non in auferendi, sed dandi casu accipiendas esse contenderit, nec sic tamen Felii commentum extundet, aut Afrorum praesulum auctoritatem pontificiae hic aequiparatam probabit, cum sensus tum esse possit: nisi forte pauci isti schismatici existiment, auctoritatem Afrorum antistitum minorem esse, quam ut eorum sententiae secundum ea, quae de appellationibus decreta fuerunt, se subjicere debeant. Istud certum est, ibidem nullam Africanorum sedium cum romana cathedra comparationem institui, Fortunatumque et ejus sectatores, desperatos et perditos dici propter haeresim ac scelera sua, non quia ad Pontificem appellaverant."]];